window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-23"]=[{"id":"q3-105-109","q":"3:105–109","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:105–109 · Division after proof, the illumination of faces and the mercy of salvation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 391–392","cairo":"I:391–392","source_page_first":391,"source_page_last":392,"ar":"وَلا تَكُونُوا كَالَّذِينَ تَفَرَّقُوا وَاخْتَلَفُوا مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَأُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ (105) يَوْمَ تَبْيَضُّ وُجُوهٌ وَتَسْوَدُّ وُجُوهٌ فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ اسْوَدَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ أَكَفَرْتُمْ بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ فَذُوقُوا الْعَذابَ بِما كُنْتُمْ تَكْفُرُونَ (106) وَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ ابْيَضَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ فَفِي رَحْمَتِ اللَّهِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (107) تِلْكَ آياتُ اللَّهِ نَتْلُوها عَلَيْكَ بِالْحَقِّ وَمَا اللَّهُ يُرِيدُ ظُلْماً لِلْعالَمِينَ (108) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ (109)\nقلت: (يوم) : متعلق بالاستقرار في خبر (أولئك) ، أو باذكر محذوفة، وقوله: (أكفرتم) : محكي بقول محذوف جواب (أما) ، أي: فيقال لهم: أكفرتم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلا تَكُونُوا كاليهود والنصارى الذين (تفرقوا) في التوحيد والتنزيه، وَاخْتَلَفُوا في أحوال الآخرة. قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «افترقت اليهود على إحدى وسبعين فرقة، وافترقت النصارى على ثنتين وسبعين فرقة، وستفترق أمتي على ثلاث وسبعين فرقة، كلها في النار إلا واحدة. قيل: ومَنْ تلك الواحدة؟ قال: ما أَنَا وأَصْحابِي عَلَيْه» . وهذا الحديث أصح مما تقدم، والصحابة يروْوُن الحديث بالمعنى، فلعلَّ الأول نسي بعض الحديث. والله أعلم.\nثم إن النهي مخصوص بالتفرق في الأصول دون الفروع، لقوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «اخْتلاَفُ أُمَّتِي رَحْمَةٌ» ، ولقوله: «من اجْتَهَدَ وأصَابَ فله أجْرَانِ، ومن اجْتَهدَ وأخطَأَ فَلَهُ أجْرٌ وَاحد» .\nثم إن أهل الكتاب تفرقوا مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ أي: الآيات والحجج المبينة للحق الموجبة للاتفاق عليه، وَأُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ، يستقر لهم هذا العذاب يَوْمَ تَبْيَضُّ وُجُوهٌ المؤمنين المتقين على التوحيد، وَتَسْوَدُّ وُجُوهٌ الكافرين المتفرقين فيه، أو تبيض وجوه المخلصين وتسود وجوه المنافقين، أو تبيض وجوه أهل السنة وتسود وجوه أهل البدعة. وبياض الوجوه وسوادها كِنَايتَان عن ظهور بهجة السرور وكآبة الخوف\nفيه، وقيل: يُوسَم أهل الحق ببياض الوجه والصحيفة وإشراق البشرة وسعي النور بين يديه وبيمينه، وأهل الباطل بأضداد ذلك. فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ اسْوَدَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ فيقال لهم يومئذ: أَكَفَرْتُمْ بمحمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- بعد ظهوره، بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ به قبل ظهوره، وهم اليهود أو أهل الردة، آمنوا فى حياته صلّى الله عليه وسلم وكفروا بعد موته.\nأو جميع الكفار، آمنوا في عالم الذر وأقروا على أنفسهم، ثم كفروا في عالم الشهادة. ويقال لهم أيضا: فَذُوقُوا الْعَذابَ بسبب ما كنتم (تكفرون) .\nوَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ ابْيَضَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ فَفِي رَحْمَتِ اللَّهِ أي: جَنته، هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ. وعبَّر بالرحمة عن الجنة تنبيهاً على أن المؤمن، وإنْ استغرق عمره في طاعة الله- تعالى-، لا يدخل الجنة إلا برحمة الله وفضله، وكان حق الترتيب أن يقدم حِلية المؤمنين لتقدُّم ذكرهم، لكن قصد أن يكون مطلعُ الكلام ومقطعُه حليةَ المؤمنين وثوابهم.\nتِلْكَ آياتُ اللَّهِ الواردة في وَعْده وَوَعِيدِه، نَتْلُوها عَلَيْكَ متلبسة بِالْحَقِّ لا شبهة فيها، فقد أعذر وأنذر، وَمَا اللَّهُ يُرِيدُ ظُلْماً لِلْعالَمِينَ إذ لا يحق عليه شيء فيظلم بنقصه، ولا يُمنع من شيء فيظلم بفعله، كما بيَّنه بقوله: وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ مُلكاً وخلقاً وعبيداً، فيجازي كلا بما وَعَدَه وأوْعَدَه، وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ كلها فيتصرف على وفْقِ مراده وسَابق مشيئته، لاَ يُسْئَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْئَلُونَ.\nالإشارة: قد نهى الله- تعالى- أهل الجمع عن التشبه بأهل الفرق، في اختلاف قلوبهم ووجوههم وآرائهم وأنظارهم، مِن بعد ما جاءتهم الدلائل الواضحات على طلب جمع القلب على الله، والتودد في الله، وصرف النظرة في شهود الله، وأولئك المفترقون لهم عذاب عظيم، وأيّ عذاب أعظم من الحجاب؟ يوم تبيض وجوه العارفين، فتكون كالشمس الضاحية، يسرحون في الجنان حيث شاءوا، وتسود وجوه الجاهلين لما يعتريها من الندم، وسوادها باعتبار وجوه العارفين في النقص عنها، وإن كانت مُبْيَضَّةً بنور الإيمان، لكن فاتهم نور الإحسان، فيقال: أكفرتم بالخصوصية في زمانكم، بعد إيمانكم بها فيمن سلف قبلكم؟ فذوقوا عذاب القطيعة عن شهود الحبيب في كل حين، وأما الذين ابيضت وجوههم وأشرقت بنور البقاء، ففي رحمة الله، أي: جنة المعارف فِي مَقْعَدِ صِدْقٍ عِنْدَ مَلِيكٍ مُقْتَدِرٍ، فقد اتضحت الطريق، وظهرت أعلام التحقيق، لكن الهداية بيد الله، كما أنَّ الأمور كلها بيده، يهدي مَن يشاء ويضل من يشاء، وَما رَبُّكَ بِظَلَّامٍ لِلْعَبِيدِ. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم مدح الأمة المحمدية بامتثال ما أمرها به من الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلا تَكُونُوا كَالَّذِينَ تَفَرَّقُوا وَاخْتَلَفُوا مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَأُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ (105) يَوْمَ تَبْيَضُّ وُجُوهٌ وَتَسْوَدُّ وُجُوهٌ فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ اسْوَدَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ أَكَفَرْتُمْ بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ فَذُوقُوا الْعَذابَ بِما كُنْتُمْ تَكْفُرُونَ (106) وَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ ابْيَضَّتْ وُجُوهُهُمْ فَفِي رَحْمَتِ اللَّهِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (107) تِلْكَ آياتُ اللَّهِ نَتْلُوها عَلَيْكَ بِالْحَقِّ وَمَا اللَّهُ يُرِيدُ ظُلْماً لِلْعالَمِينَ (108) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ (109)","quran_en":"105. Do not be like those who split apart and differed after clear proofs had come to them. For them is a mighty punishment. 106. On the Day when some faces become white and others become black: to those whose faces turn black it will be said, “Did you disbelieve after your faith? Taste the punishment, then, for your disbelief.” 107. Those whose faces become white will be in God’s mercy, abiding there forever. 108. These are God’s signs, which We recite to you in truth; God does not desire injustice for the worlds. 109. To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth; to God all affairs are returned.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The position of “the Day” and an omitted speech formula","html":"<p>I say: “The Day” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يوم<\/bdi>) depends on the implied state of subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستقرار<\/bdi>) in the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) of “those” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أولئك<\/bdi>), or on an unexpressed command “remember” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اذكر<\/bdi>) with [its governing expression] omitted. As for “Did you disbelieve?” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أكفرتم<\/bdi>), it is a quotation introduced by an unexpressed verb of saying that constitutes the answer to ammā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أما<\/bdi>): that is, “It will be said to them: Did you disbelieve?”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “The Day” ( يوم ) depends on the implied state of subsistence ( الاستقرار ) in the predicate ( خبر ) of “those” ( أولئك ), or on an unexpressed command “remember” ( اذكر ) with [its governing expression] omitted. As for “Did you disbelieve?” ( أكفرتم ), it is a quotation introduced by an unexpressed verb of saying that constitutes the answer to ammā ( أما ): that is, “It will be said to them: Did you disbelieve?”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Division in fundamentals versus plurality in derivative judgments","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Do not be like” the Jews and Christians, “those who split apart” concerning divine unity and God’s transcendence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد والتنزيه<\/bdi>), “and differed” about the states of the Hereafter. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is cited as saying: “The Jews divided into seventy-one sects, the Christians into seventy-two, and my community will divide into seventy-three; all are in the Fire except one.” Asked which one it was, he replied: “What I and my Companions are upon.” Ibn ʿAjība says that this version is sounder than the version quoted earlier, and adds that the Companions transmitted hadith according to its meaning, so perhaps the first transmitter forgot part of it. And God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N076\">[XIV-N076]<\/a><\/p><p>He then says the prohibition concerns division over fundamentals (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأصول<\/bdi>), not disagreements over derivative matters (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفروع<\/bdi>), adducing the saying “Disagreement within my community is a mercy,” as well as the saying that the one who exercises legal effort (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اجتهاد<\/bdi>) and reaches the correct conclusion receives two rewards, while one who exercises it and errs receives one. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The first saying’s status is disputed and should not be treated as an authenticated Prophetic report simply because Ibn ʿAjība quotes it.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N077\">[XIV-N077]<\/a><\/p><p>The People of the Book divided “after clear proofs had come to them,” meaning verses and demonstrations revealing the truth and demanding agreement upon it. “For them is a mighty punishment”: this punishment shall abide for them “on the Day when some faces become white,” those of believers who were mindful of God and upheld unity; “and some faces become black,” those of unbelievers divided concerning unity. Or the whitened faces are those of the sincere (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المخلصين<\/bdi>), and the darkened faces those of the hypocrites (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنافقين<\/bdi>); or the former are those who follow the Sunna and the latter the people of innovation. Whiteness and blackness of faces may be metonymies (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كنايتان<\/bdi>) for the manifest radiance of happiness and the gloom of fear. Another interpretation takes the marks literally: the people of truth are distinguished by whitened faces and records, bright complexions and light moving before them and on their right, while the people of falsehood receive the corresponding opposites. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N078\">[XIV-N078]<\/a><\/p><p>“As for those whose faces turn black,” it is said to them on that Day: “Did you disbelieve in Muḥammad,” blessings and peace be upon him, after his appearance, “after your faith” in him before his appearance? These are the Jews; or it refers to apostates (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الردة<\/bdi>) who believed during his life and disbelieved after his death. Or it means all unbelievers: they believed and acknowledged [God] in the realm of particles (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الذر<\/bdi>), then disbelieved in the realm of visible testimony (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الشهادة<\/bdi>). To them it is said: “Taste the punishment for what you used to disbelieve.”<\/p><p>“As for those whose faces become white, they will be in God’s mercy,” that is, His Garden, “abiding there forever.” He calls the Garden “mercy” to indicate that, even if a believer spends his whole life obeying God Most High, he enters the Garden only through God’s mercy and favour. One might have expected the adornment of the believers to come first because they were mentioned first, but [the passage] is designed so that both the opening and close of the discussion are concerned with the believers’ adornment and reward.<\/p><p>“These are God’s signs,” concerning His promise and warning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوعد والوعيد<\/bdi>); “We recite them to you in truth,” without uncertainty. He has made the excuse impossible and given warning. “God does not desire injustice for the worlds,” since nothing is incumbent upon Him such that deprivation of it would wrong someone; and nothing is forbidden to Him such that His doing it would be unjust. He clarifies this by saying: “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth,” by ownership, creation and servitude. He recompenses each according to what He has promised or threatened. “To God all affairs are returned”: He disposes them according to His will and His prior choice. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned” [21:23]. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N079\">[XIV-N079]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Do not be like” the Jews and Christians, “those who split apart” concerning divine unity and God’s transcendence ( التوحيد والتنزيه ), “and differed” about the states of the Hereafter. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is cited as saying: “The Jews divided into seventy-one sects, the Christians into seventy-two, and my community will divide into seventy-three; all are in the Fire except one.” Asked which one it was, he replied: “What I and my Companions are upon.” Ibn ʿAjība says that this version is sounder than the version quoted earlier, and adds that the Companions transmitted hadith according to its meaning, so perhaps the first transmitter forgot part of it. And God knows best. [XIV-N076] He then says the prohibition concerns division over fundamentals ( الأصول ), not disagreements over derivative matters ( الفروع ), adducing the saying “Disagreement within my community is a mercy,” as well as the saying that the one who exercises legal effort ( اجتهاد ) and reaches the correct conclusion receives two rewards, while one who exercises it and errs receives one. [Translator’s note: The first saying’s status is disputed and should not be treated as an authenticated Prophetic report simply because Ibn ʿAjība quotes it.] [XIV-N077] The People of the Book divided “after clear proofs had come to them,” meaning verses and demonstrations revealing the truth and demanding agreement upon it. “For them is a mighty punishment”: this punishment shall abide for them “on the Day when some faces become white,” those of believers who were mindful of God and upheld unity; “and some faces become black,” those of unbelievers divided concerning unity. Or the whitened faces are those of the sincere ( المخلصين ), and the darkened faces those of the hypocrites ( المنافقين ); or the former are those who follow the Sunna and the latter the people of innovation. Whiteness and blackness of faces may be metonymies ( كنايتان ) for the manifest radiance of happiness and the gloom of fear. Another interpretation takes the marks literally: the people of truth are distinguished by whitened faces and records, bright complexions and light moving before them and on their right, while the people of falsehood receive the corresponding opposites. [XIV-N078] “As for those whose faces turn black,” it is said to them on that Day: “Did you disbelieve in Muḥammad,” blessings and peace be upon him, after his appearance, “after your faith” in him before his appearance? These are the Jews; or it refers to apostates ( أهل الردة ) who believed during his life and disbelieved after his death. Or it means all unbelievers: they believed and acknowledged [God] in the realm of particles ( عالم الذر ), then disbelieved in the realm of visible testimony ( عالم الشهادة ). To them it is said: “Taste the punishment for what you used to disbelieve.” “As for those whose faces become white, they will be in God’s mercy,” that is, His Garden, “abiding there forever.” He calls the Garden “mercy” to indicate that, even if a believer spends his whole life obeying God Most High, he enters the Garden only through God’s mercy and favour. One might have expected the adornment of the believers to come first because they were mentioned first, but [the passage] is designed so that both the opening and close of the discussion are concerned with the believers’ adornment and reward. “These are God’s signs,” concerning His promise and warning ( الوعد والوعيد ); “We recite them to you in truth,” without uncertainty. He has made the excuse impossible and given warning. “God does not desire injustice for the worlds,” since nothing is incumbent upon Him such that deprivation of it would wrong someone; and nothing is forbidden to Him such that His doing it would be unjust. He clarifies this by saying: “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth,” by ownership, creation and servitude. He recompenses each according to what He has promised or threatened. “To God all affairs are returned”: He disposes them according to His will and His prior choice. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned” [21:23]. [XIV-N079]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The faces of realization and the pain of veiling","html":"<p>God Most High has forbidden the people of gathering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الجمع<\/bdi>) to resemble the people of separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الفرق<\/bdi>), in the differing of their hearts, their faces, their opinions and their ways of seeing, after clear proofs have come to them concerning gathering the heart upon God, affection for God’s sake, and directing the gaze towards the witnessing of God. For those divided people there is a great punishment—and what punishment is greater than the veil (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحجاب<\/bdi>)?<\/p><p>On the Day when the faces of the knowers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارفين<\/bdi>) become white, like the brilliant sun, they roam in the gardens wherever they wish. The faces of the ignorant darken with regret. Their darkness is relative to the faces of the knowers: they may be white with the light of faith (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نور الإيمان<\/bdi>), but they lack the light of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نور الإحسان<\/bdi>). It is said to them: “Did you disbelieve in special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) in your own time after believing that it existed among those before you?” Taste, then, the punishment of severance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القطيعة<\/bdi>) from witnessing the Beloved at every moment. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N080\">[XIV-N080]<\/a><\/p><p>As for those whose faces become white and shine with the light of subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نور البقاء<\/bdi>), they are in God’s mercy: that is, the Garden of gnoses (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة المعارف<\/bdi>), “in a seat of truth, in the presence of an all-powerful Sovereign” [54:55]. The path has been made clear and the waymarks of realization manifest, but guidance is in God’s hand, just as all affairs lie in His hand. He guides whom He wills and leaves astray whom He wills. “Your Lord is not unjust to His servants” [41:46]. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"God Most High has forbidden the people of gathering ( أهل الجمع ) to resemble the people of separation ( أهل الفرق ), in the differing of their hearts, their faces, their opinions and their ways of seeing, after clear proofs have come to them concerning gathering the heart upon God, affection for God’s sake, and directing the gaze towards the witnessing of God. For those divided people there is a great punishment—and what punishment is greater than the veil ( الحجاب )? On the Day when the faces of the knowers ( العارفين ) become white, like the brilliant sun, they roam in the gardens wherever they wish. The faces of the ignorant darken with regret. Their darkness is relative to the faces of the knowers: they may be white with the light of faith ( نور الإيمان ), but they lack the light of spiritual excellence ( نور الإحسان ). It is said to them: “Did you disbelieve in special spiritual distinction ( الخصوصية ) in your own time after believing that it existed among those before you?” Taste, then, the punishment of severance ( القطيعة ) from witnessing the Beloved at every moment. [XIV-N080] As for those whose faces become white and shine with the light of subsistence ( نور البقاء ), they are in God’s mercy: that is, the Garden of gnoses ( جنة المعارف ), “in a seat of truth, in the presence of an all-powerful Sovereign” [54:55]. The path has been made clear and the waymarks of realization manifest, but guidance is in God’s hand, just as all affairs lie in His hand. He guides whom He wills and leaves astray whom He wills. “Your Lord is not unjust to His servants” [41:46]. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then praised the Muhammadan community for obeying His command to enjoin right and forbid wrong, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then praised the Muhammadan community for obeying His command to enjoin right and forbid wrong, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6357,"arabic_characters":4034},{"id":"q3-110-112","q":"3:110–112","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:110–112 · The best community, degrees of spiritual knowledge and protection under covenant","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 392–396","cairo":"I:392–396","source_page_first":392,"source_page_last":396,"ar":"كُنْتُمْ خَيْرَ أُمَّةٍ أُخْرِجَتْ لِلنَّاسِ تَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَتَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ ...\nقلت: (كان) : على بابها من الدلالة على المُضِيِّ، أي: كنتم في اللوح المحفوظ، أو في علم الله، أو فيما بين الأمم المتقدمة، أو: صلة، أي: أنتم خير أمة، و (للناس) : يتعلق بأخرجت، أو بكنتم، أي: كنتم خير الناس للناس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لأمة نبينا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم: كُنْتُمْ في سابق علمي خَيْرَ أُمَّةٍ ظهرت لِلنَّاسِ تجيئون بهم إلى الجنة بالسلاسل. ثم بيَّن وجه فضلهم فقال: تَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَتَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وبجميع ما يجب الإيمان به.\nوقد ورد في مدح هذه الأمة المحمدية أحاديث، منها: قوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «حُرِّمتْ الجنةُ على الأنبياء حتى أدخلها أنا، وحُرّمتْ الجنة على الأُمَمِ حتى تدخلها أمتي» . ومنها قوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «أمتي أمةٌ مَرْحُومَةٌ، إذَا كَانَ يَوْمُ القِيامةِ أَعطى الله كُلَّ رَجُلٍ مِن هذه الأمة رجُلاً فيقال: هذَا فِدَاؤُكَ مِنَ النَّارِ» .\nوعن أنس قال: «خرجت مع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فإذا صوت يجيىء من شِعْبِ، فقال: يا أنس: قُمْ فانظرْ ما هذا الصوت، فانطلقت فإذا برجلٍ يُصلّي إلى شجرة، ويقول: اللهم اجعلني من أمة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم، الأمة المرحومة، المغفور لها، المستجاب لها، المتاب عليها، فأتيت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فأخبرته، فقال: انطلق، فقل له: إن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقرئك السلام، ويقول لك: من أنت؟ فأتيته، فأعلمته ما قال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فقال: اقرأ مني السلام على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، وقل له: أخوك الخضر يقول لك: ادع الله أن يجعلني من أمتك المرحومة المغفور لها(1)» . وقيل لعيسى بن مريم: هل بعد هذه الأمة أمة؟ قال: نعم، أمة أحمد. قيل: وما أمة أحمد؟ قال: علماء، حكماء، أبرار أتقياء، كأنهم من الفقه أنبياء، يرضون باليسير من الرزق، ويرضى الله عنهم باليسير من العمل، يدخلهم الجنة بشهادة أن لا إله إلا الله. هـ.\nوليس أولها أولى بالمدح من آخرها، لقوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «أمتي كالمطر، لا يدرى أولهُ خيرٌ أو آخرُه» ؟ وفي خبر آخر عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم قال: «اشتقْتُ إلى إخواني، فقال أصحابه: نحن إخوانُك يا رسول الله، فقال: أنتم أصْحَابي، إخْواني: ناس يأتُون بعدي، يُؤمنون بي ولم يَرَوْنِي، يَوَدُّ أحدُهم لو يَرَاني بجميع ما يَمْلِكُ. يَعْدِلُ عملُ أحدهم سبعين منكم. قالوا:\nمِنْهم يا رسول الله؟ قال: منكم. قالوا: ولِمَ ذلك يا رسول الله؟ قال: لأنكم وَجَدْتُم على الخير أعواناً، وهم لم يَجِدُوا عليه أعواناً» . أو كما قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-.\nقلت: التفضيل باعتبار أجور الأعمال، وأما باعتبار اليقين والمعرفة، فالصحابة أفضل الخلق بعد الأنبياء- عليهم السلام- ويدل على هذا قوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «يعدل عمل أحدهم» ، ولم يقل إيمان أحدهم(2) .\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: كنتم يا معشر الصوفية خير أمة أخرجت للناس، تأمرون بالجمع على الله والغيبة عما سواه، وتنهونَ عن كلِّ ما يُبعد عن الله ويفرق العبدَ عن مولاه، وتؤمنون بالله وبما وعد به الله، إيمان الشهود والعيان، الذي هو مقام الإحسان. قال القشيري في رسالته: (قد جعل الله هذه الطائفة صفوة أوليائه، وفضَّلهم على الكافة من عباده بعد رسله وأنبيائه) .\nوقال الجنيد رضي الله عنه: لو نعلم أن تحت أديمِ السماء أشرف من هذا العلم الذي نتكلم فيه مع أصحابنا، لسعيت إليه ولو حبواً. هـ. وكان كثيراً ما ينشد:\nعِلْمُ التصوفِ عِلمٌ ليس يَعْرِفُهُ ... إلاَّ أَخْو فِطْنَةِ بِالحقِّ معروفُ\nوليسَ يُبْصِرهُ مَنْ ليس يَشْهدهُ ... وكيفَ يَشْهَدُ ضوءَ الشمس مكفوفُ\nوقال الشيخ الصقلي: (كلُّ من صدَّقَ بهذا العلم فهو من الخاصة، وكل من فهمه فهو من خاصة الخاصة، وكل من عبّر به وتكلم فيه فهو من النجم الذي لا يُدرك والبحر الذي لا ينزف) . وقال في الإحياء- لمَّا تكلم على معرفة الله والعلم بالله، قال: (والرتبةُ العليا في ذلك للأنبياء، ثم للأولياء العارفين، ثم للعلماء الراسخين، ثم للصالحين) . فقد قدَّم الأولياء على العلماء. قال ابن رشد: وما قاله القشيري والغزالي متفق عليه. قال: ولا يشكُّ عاقلٌ أنَّ العارفين بالله وما يجب له من الكمال، أفضل من العارفين بأحكام الله. انظر تمامه في المعيار. وقال في المباحث:\nحجّة من يرجّح الصُوفية ... على سواهم حُجَّةٌ قويَّة\nهُمْ أَتْبَعُ النَّاسِ لخيرِ النَّاس ... مِنْ سَائِرِ الأَنَامِ والأُنَاس\nثم قال:\nثُمَّ بِشَيْئَيْنِ تقومُ الحُجَّه ... أنَّهمْ قَطْعاً على المَحَجَّه(1)\nوَمَا أَتَوْا فيه بخَرْقِ الْعَادَه ... إذْ لمْ تَكُنْ لِمَنْ سِوَاهُم عَادَه\nقَدْ رَفَضُوا الآثَامَ والعُيوب ... وطَهَّرُوا الأبدَانَ والقُلُوب\nوَبَلَغُوا حقيقَةَ الإيمَان ... وانْتَهَجُوا منَاهِجَ الإحْسَان\nثم دعا أهل الكتاب إلى الإيمان، وهوَّن أمرهم، فقال:\n... وَلَوْ آمَنَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ لَكانَ خَيْراً لَهُمْ مِنْهُمُ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ وَأَكْثَرُهُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ (110) لَنْ يَضُرُّوكُمْ إِلاَّ أَذىً وَإِنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ يُوَلُّوكُمُ الْأَدْبارَ ثُمَّ لاَ يُنْصَرُونَ (111) ضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ أَيْنَ مَا ثُقِفُوا إِلاَّ بِحَبْلٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَحَبْلٍ مِنَ النَّاسِ وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الْمَسْكَنَةُ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كانُوا يَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَيَقْتُلُونَ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ ذلِكَ بِما عَصَوْا وَكانُوا يَعْتَدُونَ (112)\nقلت: الاستثناء في قوله (إلا بحبل) : من أعم الأحوال، أي: ضربت عليهم الذلة في جميع الأحوال، إلا متلبسين بذمة من الله وذمة من الناس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَوْ آمَنَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ إيمانا كائنا كإيمانكم، لَكانَ خَيْراً لَهُمْ مما هم عليه. وليس أهل الكتاب سواء، بل مِنْهُمُ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ كعبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه، وَأَكْثَرُهُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ المتمردون في الكفر والفسوق، فلا يهولكم أمرهم، فإنهم لَنْ يَضُرُّوكُمْ إلا ضرراً يسيراً كأذى باللسان من عيب وسب وتحريش بينكم، ولا قدرة لهم على القتال، وَإِنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ ينهزموا، ويُوَلُّوكُمُ الْأَدْبارَ ثُمَّ لا يُنْصَرُونَ أبداً عليكم.\nوهذه الآية من المُغيبات التي وافقها الواقع، إذ كان كذلك في بني قريظة والنضير وبني قينقاع وخيبر، فلم تُرْفع لهم راية أبداً، بل ضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ والخزي والهوان، أي: أحاطت بهم إحاطة البيت المضروب على أهله، أو لزمتهم لزوم الدرهم المضروب لضربه، فلا تنفك عنهم أَيْنَ ما ثُقِفُوا ووُجدوا، فلا يأمنون إِلَّا بِحَبْلٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ أي: بسبب عهد من الله، وهو عقد الذمة التي أمر الله بها، إذا أدوا الجزية للمسلمين، فلهم حرمة بسبب هذا العقد، فلا يجوز التعرض لهم في مال ولا دم ولا أهل، وَحَبْلٍ مِنَ النَّاسِ، وهو عقد الذمة التي يعقدها مع الكفار إذا كانوا تحت ذمتهم. والحاصل أن الذلة لازمة لهم(1) فلا يأمنون إلا تحت الذمة، إما من المسلمين وإما من الكفار. وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ أي: انقلبوا به مستحقين له، وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الْمَسْكَنَةُ أي: أحاطت بهم، فاليهود في الغالب فقراء مساكين، لأن قلوبهم خاوية من اليقين، فالفقر والجزع لازم لهم، ولو ملكوا الدنيا بأجمعها.\nذلِكَ الذل والمسكنة والبواء بالغضب بسبب أنهم كانُوا يَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ المنزلة على رسوله، أو الدالة على توحيده، وَيَقْتُلُونَ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ بل ظلماً وعدواناً، ذلك الكفر بسبب عصيانهم واعتدائهم حدود الله، فإن الإصرار على الصغائر يُفضي إلى الكبائر، والإصرار على الكبائر يؤدي إلى الكفر لأن المعاصي بريد الكفر، والعياذ بالله.\nالإشارة: ولو آمن أهل العلم الظاهر بطريق الخصوص، وحطوا رؤوسهم لأهل الخصوصية لكان خيرا لهم، لتتسع عليهم دائرة العلوم، وتفتح لهم مخازن الفهوم، منهم من يقر بوجود الخصوصية، ويعجز عن حمل شروطها، وأكثرهم ينكرونها ويحتجون لأنفسهم بقول من قال: انقطعت التربية في القرن الثامن، فيموتون مصرين على الإنكار والعصيان، فلن يضركم إنكارهم أيها الفقراء، فإنهم لا قدرة لهم عليكم، للرعاية التي أحاطت بكم، إلا أذى بلسانهم، وعلى تقدير لحوق ضررهم في الظاهر، فإن الله يُغيِّبْ ألمّ ذلك عنكم في الباطن، كما شاهدناه من بعض الفقراء، وإن يُهددوكم بالقتل والجلاء، فإن الله لا يَنْصُرُهم في الغالب.\nقلت: وقد هددونا بالضرب والرفع إلى السلطان والجلاء إلى برِّ النصارى، فلم يقدروا على شيء من ذلك، وقد وقع ذلك لبعض الصوفية زيادةً في شرفهم وعزّهم، فالمنكر على الصوفية(2) لا يزال في هَمٍّ وغمٍّ وذُلٍّ ومسكنة، لخراب باطنه من نور اليقين. فإنَّ الانتقاد على الأولياء جناية واعتقادهم عناية، فإن استمر على أذاهم كان عاقبته سوء الخاتمة، فيبوء بغضب من الله بسبب اعتدائه على أولياء الله، «ومن آذى لي وليا فقد أذن بالحرب» ، رزقنا الله الأدب معهم، وأماتنا على محبتهم، آمين.\nولمّا كان من اليهود من أسلم وحسن إسلامه استثناه الله تعالى، فقال:","quran_ar":"كُنْتُمْ خَيْرَ أُمَّةٍ أُخْرِجَتْ لِلنَّاسِ تَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَتَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَلَوْ آمَنَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ لَكانَ خَيْراً لَهُمْ مِنْهُمُ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ وَأَكْثَرُهُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ (110) لَنْ يَضُرُّوكُمْ إِلاَّ أَذىً وَإِنْ يُقاتِلُوكُمْ يُوَلُّوكُمُ الْأَدْبارَ ثُمَّ لاَ يُنْصَرُونَ (111) ضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ أَيْنَ مَا ثُقِفُوا إِلاَّ بِحَبْلٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَحَبْلٍ مِنَ النَّاسِ وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الْمَسْكَنَةُ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كانُوا يَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَيَقْتُلُونَ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ ذلِكَ بِما عَصَوْا وَكانُوا يَعْتَدُونَ (112)","quran_en":"110. You were the best community brought forth for humankind: you enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and believe in God. Had the People of the Book believed, it would have been better for them. Among them are believers, but most of them are defiantly disobedient. 111. They will not harm you, except by causing hurt; and if they fight you, they will turn their backs upon you, and then they will not be helped. 112. Humiliation has been laid upon them wherever they are found, unless they hold to a covenant from God and a covenant from people. They have incurred wrath from God, and poverty has been laid upon them. This is because they rejected God’s signs and killed prophets without right. That was because they disobeyed and kept transgressing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The force of “you were” and the beneficiaries of goodness","html":"<p>I say: “You were” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كُنْتُمْ<\/bdi>): kāna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كان<\/bdi>) may retain its normal force indicating the past (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المضي<\/bdi>): you were [so designated] in the Preserved Tablet (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اللوح المحفوظ<\/bdi>), or in God’s knowledge, or among earlier peoples. Or it may be an expletive connective (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صلة<\/bdi>), meaning simply “You are the best community.” “For humankind” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للنّاس<\/bdi>) may depend upon “brought forth” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أخرجت<\/bdi>), or upon “you were” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كنتم<\/bdi>), yielding “You were the best of people <i>for<\/i> people.”<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-15\">[R07-15]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “You were” ( كُنْتُمْ ): kāna ( كان ) may retain its normal force indicating the past ( المضي ): you were [so designated] in the Preserved Tablet ( اللوح المحفوظ ), or in God’s knowledge, or among earlier peoples. Or it may be an expletive connective ( صلة ), meaning simply “You are the best community.” “For humankind” ( للنّاس ) may depend upon “brought forth” ( أخرجت ), or upon “you were” ( كنتم ), yielding “You were the best of people for people.” [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-15] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The virtue of the Muhammadan community","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says to the community of our Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him: “You were,” in My prior knowledge, “the best community brought forth for humankind,” leading people into Paradise even in chains. He then states the reason for their excellence: “You enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and believe in God,” and in everything it is obligatory to believe.<\/p><p>Reports praising the Muhammadan community include the Prophet’s words, as cited here: “Paradise was withheld from the prophets until I enter it, and withheld from the communities until my community enters.” And: “My community is a community shown mercy. On the Day of Resurrection God gives each man of this community another man, and it is said: This is your ransom from the Fire.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N081\">[XIV-N081]<\/a><\/p><p>Anas is reported as saying: “I went out with the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, and a voice was coming from a mountain pass. He said: ‘Anas, get up and find out what this voice is.’ I went, and there was a man praying toward a tree and saying: ‘O God, make me one of Muḥammad’s community, the community that receives mercy, is forgiven, is answered and whose repentance is accepted.’ I told the Prophet. He said: ‘Go and tell him that God’s Messenger sends his greeting and asks: Who are you?’ I conveyed the message. He said: ‘Send my greeting to God’s Messenger and say to him: Your brother al-Khiḍr asks you to pray that God make me a member of your community—the community shown mercy and forgiven.’” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N082\">[XIV-N082]<\/a><\/p><p>It was said to Jesus son of Mary: “Will there be a community after this one?” He answered: “Yes, the community of Aḥmad.” Asked about its character, he said: “They are scholars, wise ones, righteous and Godfearing, like prophets in their understanding of religion. They are content with a little provision, and God is content with a little work from them. He admits them into Paradise by their testimony that there is no god but God.” End of cited account. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N082\">[XIV-N082]<\/a><\/p><p>The earliest members of the community do not have an exclusive claim to praise over its later members, in view of the saying: “My community is like rain: one does not know whether its beginning is better or its end.” Another report describes the Prophet saying: “I long for my brothers.” His companions asked: “Are we not your brothers, Messenger of God?” He replied: “You are my companions. My brothers are people who will come after me, believe in me without having seen me, and one of them would desire to see me even in exchange for everything he owns. The work of one of them is equal to the work of seventy of you.” They asked: “Of them or of us?” He said: “Of you.” “Why is that, Messenger of God?” “Because you found helpers in goodness and they found no helpers in it,” or words to that effect, as Ibn ʿAjība himself qualifies the quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N083\">[XIV-N083]<\/a><\/p><p>I say: This preference concerns the rewards of deeds. Regarding certainty and gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اليقين والمعرفة<\/bdi>), the Companions are the best of creation after the prophets, peace be upon them. The evidence is that the Prophet spoke of one person’s <i>work<\/i> being equal, not of that person’s faith being equal. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says to the community of our Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him: “You were,” in My prior knowledge, “the best community brought forth for humankind,” leading people into Paradise even in chains. He then states the reason for their excellence: “You enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and believe in God,” and in everything it is obligatory to believe. Reports praising the Muhammadan community include the Prophet’s words, as cited here: “Paradise was withheld from the prophets until I enter it, and withheld from the communities until my community enters.” And: “My community is a community shown mercy. On the Day of Resurrection God gives each man of this community another man, and it is said: This is your ransom from the Fire.” [XIV-N081] Anas is reported as saying: “I went out with the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, and a voice was coming from a mountain pass. He said: ‘Anas, get up and find out what this voice is.’ I went, and there was a man praying toward a tree and saying: ‘O God, make me one of Muḥammad’s community, the community that receives mercy, is forgiven, is answered and whose repentance is accepted.’ I told the Prophet. He said: ‘Go and tell him that God’s Messenger sends his greeting and asks: Who are you?’ I conveyed the message. He said: ‘Send my greeting to God’s Messenger and say to him: Your brother al-Khiḍr asks you to pray that God make me a member of your community—the community shown mercy and forgiven.’” [XIV-N082] It was said to Jesus son of Mary: “Will there be a community after this one?” He answered: “Yes, the community of Aḥmad.” Asked about its character, he said: “They are scholars, wise ones, righteous and Godfearing, like prophets in their understanding of religion. They are content with a little provision, and God is content with a little work from them. He admits them into Paradise by their testimony that there is no god but God.” End of cited account. [XIV-N082] The earliest members of the community do not have an exclusive claim to praise over its later members, in view of the saying: “My community is like rain: one does not know whether its beginning is better or its end.” Another report describes the Prophet saying: “I long for my brothers.” His companions asked: “Are we not your brothers, Messenger of God?” He replied: “You are my companions. My brothers are people who will come after me, believe in me without having seen me, and one of them would desire to see me even in exchange for everything he owns. The work of one of them is equal to the work of seventy of you.” They asked: “Of them or of us?” He said: “Of you.” “Why is that, Messenger of God?” “Because you found helpers in goodness and they found no helpers in it,” or words to that effect, as Ibn ʿAjība himself qualifies the quotation. [XIV-N083] I say: This preference concerns the rewards of deeds. Regarding certainty and gnosis ( اليقين والمعرفة ), the Companions are the best of creation after the prophets, peace be upon them. The evidence is that the Prophet spoke of one person’s work being equal, not of that person’s faith being equal. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The Sufis as a community calling to direct witnessing","html":"<p>“You were,” O company of Sufis, “the best community brought forth for humankind”: you enjoin gathering upon God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمع على الله<\/bdi>) and being absent to everything other than Him; you forbid anything that distances one from God and divides the servant from his Master. You believe in God and in what He promised with the faith of witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إيمان الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>), the station of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī says in his <i>Risāla<\/i>: “God has made this company the elect among His friends and preferred them to all His servants after His messengers and prophets.” Al-Junayd said: “If we knew of a knowledge more noble, beneath the canopy of the sky, than the knowledge about which we speak with our companions, I would strive for it even if I had to crawl.” He often recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The science of Sufism is a science known only<br/>To the discerning one who is recognized through the Real;<br/>One who does not witness it does not behold it—<br/>How should a blind man witness the sunlight?<\/blockquote><p>Al-Shaykh al-Ṣiqillī said: “Whoever believes this knowledge belongs to the elect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخاصة<\/bdi>); whoever understands it belongs to the elect of the elect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خاصة الخاصة<\/bdi>); and whoever can express and speak of it is like a star that cannot be reached or a sea that cannot be drained.”<\/p><p>In the <i>Iḥyāʾ<\/i>, when speaking of knowing God and the science of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة الله والعلم بالله<\/bdi>), al-Ghazālī says: “The highest rank in that belongs to the prophets, then to the friends of God who know Him, then to scholars deeply established in knowledge, then to the righteous.” He thereby places the friends of God before the scholars. Ibn Rushd says, according to the source cited by Ibn ʿAjība, that what al-Qushayrī and al-Ghazālī say is agreed upon. He adds: “No rational person doubts that those who know God and what perfection must be attributed to Him are better than those who know God’s legal rulings.” Consult the complete discussion in <i>al-Miʿyār<\/i>. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N084\">[XIV-N084]<\/a><\/p><p>The author of <i>al-Mabāḥith<\/i> says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The argument that sets the Sufis above others<br/>Is a strong argument indeed.<br/>They follow, more than all other people,<br/>The best of all humankind.<\/blockquote><p>And then:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Two further things establish the proof:<br/>That they surely follow the clear road,<br/>And what they display of extraordinary gifts,<br/>Uncustomary among other people.<br/>They have renounced sins and faults,<br/>And purified their bodies and hearts.<br/>They have attained the reality of faith<br/>And walked the pathways of spiritual excellence.<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"“You were,” O company of Sufis, “the best community brought forth for humankind”: you enjoin gathering upon God ( الجمع على الله ) and being absent to everything other than Him; you forbid anything that distances one from God and divides the servant from his Master. You believe in God and in what He promised with the faith of witnessing and direct vision ( إيمان الشهود والعيان ), the station of spiritual excellence ( مقام الإحسان ). Al-Qushayrī says in his Risāla : “God has made this company the elect among His friends and preferred them to all His servants after His messengers and prophets.” Al-Junayd said: “If we knew of a knowledge more noble, beneath the canopy of the sky, than the knowledge about which we speak with our companions, I would strive for it even if I had to crawl.” He often recited: The science of Sufism is a science known only To the discerning one who is recognized through the Real; One who does not witness it does not behold it— How should a blind man witness the sunlight? Al-Shaykh al-Ṣiqillī said: “Whoever believes this knowledge belongs to the elect ( الخاصة ); whoever understands it belongs to the elect of the elect ( خاصة الخاصة ); and whoever can express and speak of it is like a star that cannot be reached or a sea that cannot be drained.” In the Iḥyāʾ , when speaking of knowing God and the science of God ( معرفة الله والعلم بالله ), al-Ghazālī says: “The highest rank in that belongs to the prophets, then to the friends of God who know Him, then to scholars deeply established in knowledge, then to the righteous.” He thereby places the friends of God before the scholars. Ibn Rushd says, according to the source cited by Ibn ʿAjība, that what al-Qushayrī and al-Ghazālī say is agreed upon. He adds: “No rational person doubts that those who know God and what perfection must be attributed to Him are better than those who know God’s legal rulings.” Consult the complete discussion in al-Miʿyār . [XIV-N084] The author of al-Mabāḥith says: The argument that sets the Sufis above others Is a strong argument indeed. They follow, more than all other people, The best of all humankind. And then: Two further things establish the proof: That they surely follow the clear road, And what they display of extraordinary gifts, Uncustomary among other people. They have renounced sins and faults, And purified their bodies and hearts. They have attained the reality of faith And walked the pathways of spiritual excellence."},{"type":"language","title":"The covenant as an exception to all circumstances","html":"<p>He then called the People of the Book to faith and made light of their power, saying: “Had [the People of the Book] believed …”<\/p><p>I say: The exception “except by a cord” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إلا بحبل<\/bdi>) is from the most general class of circumstances (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أعم الأحوال<\/bdi>): humiliation is laid upon them in every condition save when they are clothed in a protection granted by God and a protection granted by people.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then called the People of the Book to faith and made light of their power, saying: “Had [the People of the Book] believed …” I say: The exception “except by a cord” ( إلا بحبل ) is from the most general class of circumstances ( أعم الأحوال ): humiliation is laid upon them in every condition save when they are clothed in a protection granted by God and a protection granted by people."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Security, limits on harm and the cause of moral deterioration","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Had the People of the Book believed” with a belief such as yours, “it would have been better for them” than their present condition. Yet they are not all alike: “Among them are believers,” such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, “but most of them are defiantly disobedient,” rebellious in unbelief and transgression.<\/p><p>Do not be awed by them. “They will not harm you, except by causing hurt”—slight harm from the tongue, such as reviling, insult and stirring discord among you. They have no power to fight; “if they fight you” they will be defeated and “turn their backs on you, and then will not be helped” against you.<\/p><p>Ibn ʿAjība calls this a prediction of unseen events that subsequently matched what occurred with Banū Qurayẓa, Banū al-Naḍīr, Banū Qaynuqāʿ and Khaybar. In his account, they never raised a victorious standard. Instead, “humiliation has been laid upon them,” shame and abasement surrounding them like a tent spread over its occupants—or adhering to them like the imprint of a stamped coin—“wherever they are found.” They enjoy security only “through a cord from God,” meaning a covenant or guarantee from God, expressed in the dhimma contract (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عقد الذمة<\/bdi>) that God prescribed. When those under it pay the poll tax (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجزية<\/bdi>), this contract safeguards them: <strong>it is forbidden to attack their property, blood or families<\/strong>. The other is “a cord from people,” a covenant granted by other human authorities to those residing under their protection. In sum, humiliation clings to them, and they obtain security only beneath a covenant, either from Muslims or from unbelievers. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N085\">[XIV-N085]<\/a><\/p><p>“They have incurred wrath from God,” that is, they have returned deserving it; “poverty has been laid upon them,” encircling them. Jews are generally poor and needy, because their hearts are empty of certainty; poverty and anxiety cling to them, even if they possess the entire world. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: This is the commentator’s historically situated polemic.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N085\">[XIV-N085]<\/a><\/p><p>“That”—humiliation, need and incurred wrath—“is because they rejected God’s signs,” revealed to His messenger or indicating divine unity, “and killed the prophets without right,” with injustice and aggression. Their unbelief, he says, arose from disobedience and crossing God’s bounds: persistence in minor sins leads to major sins, and persistence in major sins leads to unbelief, for sins are the forerunners of unbelief. God protect us.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Had the People of the Book believed” with a belief such as yours, “it would have been better for them” than their present condition. Yet they are not all alike: “Among them are believers,” such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, “but most of them are defiantly disobedient,” rebellious in unbelief and transgression. Do not be awed by them. “They will not harm you, except by causing hurt”—slight harm from the tongue, such as reviling, insult and stirring discord among you. They have no power to fight; “if they fight you” they will be defeated and “turn their backs on you, and then will not be helped” against you. Ibn ʿAjība calls this a prediction of unseen events that subsequently matched what occurred with Banū Qurayẓa, Banū al-Naḍīr, Banū Qaynuqāʿ and Khaybar. In his account, they never raised a victorious standard. Instead, “humiliation has been laid upon them,” shame and abasement surrounding them like a tent spread over its occupants—or adhering to them like the imprint of a stamped coin—“wherever they are found.” They enjoy security only “through a cord from God,” meaning a covenant or guarantee from God, expressed in the dhimma contract ( عقد الذمة ) that God prescribed. When those under it pay the poll tax ( الجزية ), this contract safeguards them: it is forbidden to attack their property, blood or families . The other is “a cord from people,” a covenant granted by other human authorities to those residing under their protection. In sum, humiliation clings to them, and they obtain security only beneath a covenant, either from Muslims or from unbelievers. [XIV-N085] “They have incurred wrath from God,” that is, they have returned deserving it; “poverty has been laid upon them,” encircling them. Jews are generally poor and needy, because their hearts are empty of certainty; poverty and anxiety cling to them, even if they possess the entire world. [Translator’s note: This is the commentator’s historically situated polemic.] [XIV-N085] “That”—humiliation, need and incurred wrath—“is because they rejected God’s signs,” revealed to His messenger or indicating divine unity, “and killed the prophets without right,” with injustice and aggression. Their unbelief, he says, arose from disobedience and crossing God’s bounds: persistence in minor sins leads to major sins, and persistence in major sins leads to unbelief, for sins are the forerunners of unbelief. God protect us."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Hostility to the friends of God and testimony from the author’s own trials","html":"<p>Had the people of outward learning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل العلم الظاهر<\/bdi>) believed in the path of particular realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق الخصوص<\/bdi>) and humbled themselves before those endowed with special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الخصوصية<\/bdi>), it would have been better for them. Their circle of knowledge would have widened and treasuries of understanding opened. Some acknowledge the existence of such particularity but lack the strength to bear its conditions. Most of them deny it and justify themselves by citing someone who said that spiritual training (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية<\/bdi>) ended in the eighth Islamic century; they die persisting in denial and disobedience.<\/p><p>Yet their denial will not harm you, O spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>): they have no power over you because of the protection surrounding you, apart from harm caused by their tongues. Even if visible harm reaches you, God will make you inwardly absent from its pain, as we ourselves have witnessed concerning some of the spiritual poor. If they threaten you with killing and exile, God will generally not help them achieve it.<\/p><p>I say: They threatened to beat us, report us to the ruler, and banish us to the land of the Christians, yet they accomplished none of it. Some Sufis have indeed undergone such treatment, which only increased their honour and dignity. One who censures the Sufis, says Ibn ʿAjība, remains in distress, sorrow, humiliation and need because his inward being is ruined of the light of certainty. Criticism of God’s friends is a wrongdoing, whereas belief in them brings care. If one persists in injuring them, one will meet a bad end and incur God’s anger by wronging His friends: “Whoever harms a friend of Mine is on notice of war.” <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: Cairo I:396 prints <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فقد أذن بالحرب<\/bdi>, without a first-person verb. This is read here in the attested sense of <i>adhina bi-<\/i>, “became aware of,” rather than silently supplying <i>ādhantuhu<\/i>, “I have notified him”; see <i>Lisān al-ʿArab<\/i>, s.v. <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أذن<\/bdi>.]<\/span> May God grant us good manners towards them and allow us to die loving them. Amen. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N086\">[XIV-N086]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Had the people of outward learning ( أهل العلم الظاهر ) believed in the path of particular realization ( طريق الخصوص ) and humbled themselves before those endowed with special spiritual distinction ( أهل الخصوصية ), it would have been better for them. Their circle of knowledge would have widened and treasuries of understanding opened. Some acknowledge the existence of such particularity but lack the strength to bear its conditions. Most of them deny it and justify themselves by citing someone who said that spiritual training ( التربية ) ended in the eighth Islamic century; they die persisting in denial and disobedience. Yet their denial will not harm you, O spiritual poor ( الفقراء ): they have no power over you because of the protection surrounding you, apart from harm caused by their tongues. Even if visible harm reaches you, God will make you inwardly absent from its pain, as we ourselves have witnessed concerning some of the spiritual poor. If they threaten you with killing and exile, God will generally not help them achieve it. I say: They threatened to beat us, report us to the ruler, and banish us to the land of the Christians, yet they accomplished none of it. Some Sufis have indeed undergone such treatment, which only increased their honour and dignity. One who censures the Sufis, says Ibn ʿAjība, remains in distress, sorrow, humiliation and need because his inward being is ruined of the light of certainty. Criticism of God’s friends is a wrongdoing, whereas belief in them brings care. If one persists in injuring them, one will meet a bad end and incur God’s anger by wronging His friends: “Whoever harms a friend of Mine is on notice of war.” [Translator’s note: Cairo I:396 prints فقد أذن بالحرب , without a first-person verb. This is read here in the attested sense of adhina bi- , “became aware of,” rather than silently supplying ādhantuhu , “I have notified him”; see Lisān al-ʿArab , s.v. أذن .] May God grant us good manners towards them and allow us to die loving them. Amen. [XIV-N086]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Since some Jews did submit and became good Muslims, God Most High distinguished them as an exception, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Since some Jews did submit and became good Muslims, God Most High distinguished them as an exception, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C393.1","page":393,"ar":"ذكره الحافظ ابن حجر بألفاظ مغايرة في الإصابة 2/122، وعزاه لابن عساكر وابن شاهين وابن عدي في الكامل.","en":"Al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar mentions it in different wording in al-Iṣāba, II:122, attributing it to Ibn ʿAsākir, Ibn Shāhīn and Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil.","anchor":"من أمتك المرحومة المغفور لها"},{"id":"C393.2","page":393,"ar":"قال الحافظ ابن حجر: الجمهور على أن فضيلة الصحبة لا يعدلها عمل، لمشاهدة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم. ثم قال: وزيادة الأجر لا يستلزم ثبوت الأفضلية المطلقة. انظر بقية كلامه في الفتح 7/9. وانظر أيضا تفسير القرطبي.","en":"Al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar says: The majority hold that no deed equals the excellence of being a Companion, because of seeing God’s Messenger—blessings and peace be upon him. He then says that a greater reward does not entail absolute superiority. See the remainder of his discussion in al-Fatḥ, VII:9; see also al-Qurṭubī’s commentary.","anchor":"ولم يقل إيمان أحدهم"},{"id":"C394.1","page":394,"ar":"المحجة: الطريق المستقيم.","en":"The maḥajja is the straight road.","anchor":"على المَحَجَّه"},{"id":"C395.1","page":395,"ar":"أقام اليهود لهم دولة بمعونة الظلمة، وحمايتهم لهم، كما فعل البريطانيون والأمريكان. لكن المسكنة لازمة لليهود ويبعث الله عليهم من يسومهم سوء العذاب، حتى مع وجودهم متحصنين داخل دولتهم.","en":"The Jews established a state for themselves with the help and protection of the unjust, as the British and Americans have provided. Yet abasement clings to the Jews, and God sends against them those who subject them to grievous punishment, even while they are fortified within their state.","anchor":"الذلة لازمة لهم"},{"id":"C396.1","page":396,"ar":"أي: الصوفية الملتزمة، لا صوفية المزمار.","en":"That is, Sufis who observe their obligations, not “Sufis of the flute.”","anchor":"فالمنكر على الصوفية"}],"english_characters":11149,"arabic_characters":7559},{"id":"q3-113-115","q":"3:113–115","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:113–115 · The upright People of the Book and the successive callings of learned people","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 396–397","cairo":"I:396–397","source_page_first":396,"source_page_last":397,"ar":"لَيْسُوا سَواءً مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ أُمَّةٌ قائِمَةٌ يَتْلُونَ آياتِ اللَّهِ آناءَ اللَّيْلِ وَهُمْ يَسْجُدُونَ (113) يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَيُسارِعُونَ فِي الْخَيْراتِ وَأُولئِكَ مِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (114) وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلَنْ تُكْفَرُوهُ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُتَّقِينَ (115)\nقلت: (قائمة) أي: مستقيمة، من أقمت العود فقام، أو قائمة بأمر الله. و (آناء الليل) : ظرف، واحده: (إنّي) ، بكسر الهمزة وسكون النون، كنحى وأنحاء، أو (إني) ، كمِعىً وأمعاء، و (لن تكفروه) أي: لن تحرموه، وعدي (كفر) إلى مفعولين لتضمنه معنى حرم أو منع.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ليس أهل الكتاب سَواءً في الكفر والعدوان، بل منهم أُمَّةٌ أي: طائفة قائِمَةٌ بالعدل مستقيمة في الدين، أو قائمة بأمر الله، أو قائمة في الصلاة يَتْلُونَ آياتِ اللَّهِ في تهجدهم آناءَ اللَّيْلِ أي: في ساعاته، وَهُمْ يَسْجُدُونَ في صلاتهم، أو في صلاة العشاء، لأن أهل الكتاب لا يصلونها، لما روى أنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أخَّرها، ثم خَرَج، فإذا الناسُ يَنْتَظُرونَها، فقال: «أبشروا فَإِنَّه لَيْسَ مِنْ أَهْل الأَرض أحدٌ يُصَلِّي في هَذِهِ السّاعَةِ غَيْرُكُم» .\nثم وصفهم بالإيمان فقال: يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَيُسارِعُونَ فِي الْخَيْراتِ، وهو عبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه ممن أسلم من اليهود، فقد وصفهم الله تعالى بخصائص لم توجد في اليهود، فإنهم منحرفون عن الحق غير متعبدين، مشركون بالله ملحدون في صفاته، يصفون اليوم الآخر بغير صفاته، مداهنون في الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر، متباطئون عن الخيرات، بخلاف ما وصف به من أسلم منهم، وَأُولئِكَ الموصوفون بتلك الصفات مِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ أي: ممن صلحت أحوالهم عند الله، واستوجبوا رضاه وثناءه، وهذه عادة الله مع خلقه، من تقرب إليه شبراً تقرب إليه ذراعاً. ولذلك قال: وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلَنْ تُكْفَرُوهُ أي: فلن تحرموا ثوابه. ولن تجحدوا جزاءه، بل يشكره لكم ويجزيكم عليه، سَمى الحرمان كفراناً كما سمى العطاء شكرا. وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُتَّقِينَ فلا يخفى عليه مقاماتهم في التقوى. وفيه إشعار بأن التقوى مبدأ الخير وأحسن الأعمال، وأن الفائزين عند الله هم أهل التقوى. رزقنا الله منها الحظ الأوفر بمنِّه. آمين.\nالإشارة: ليس أهل العلم سواء، بل منهم من جعله شبكةً يصطاد به الدنيا، يبيع دينه بعرض قليل، وهم علماء السوء وقضاة الجور، ومنهم من قرأه لله وعلَّمه لله، فأفنى عُمره في تعليمه وتقييده، ومنهم من صرف همته إلى جمعه وتأليفه، ومنهم من صرف همته إلى العمل به فالتحق بالعباد والزُّهاد، يَتْلُونَ آياتِ اللَّهِ آناءَ اللَّيْلِ وَهُمْ يَسْجُدُونَ ومنهم من حرره وحققه، ثم توجه إلى علم الباطن وصحب العارفين، فكان من المقربين، فهؤلاء كلهم يُسارِعُونَ فِي الْخَيْراتِ وَأُولئِكَ مِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ، فيقال لهم: وما تفعلوا من خير فلن تكفروه والله عليم بالمتقين.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى أضدادهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"لَيْسُوا سَواءً مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ أُمَّةٌ قائِمَةٌ يَتْلُونَ آياتِ اللَّهِ آناءَ اللَّيْلِ وَهُمْ يَسْجُدُونَ (113) يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَيُسارِعُونَ فِي الْخَيْراتِ وَأُولئِكَ مِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (114) وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلَنْ تُكْفَرُوهُ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُتَّقِينَ (115)","quran_en":"113. They are not all alike. Among the People of the Book is an upright community, reciting God’s signs through the hours of the night while they prostrate themselves. 114. They believe in God and the Last Day, enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and hasten to good deeds. They are among the righteous. 115. Whatever good you do, you shall not be denied it; God knows those who are mindful of Him.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The upright community, hours of night and “denying” recompense","html":"<p>I say: “Upright” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قائمة<\/bdi>) means straight or sound (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مستقيمة<\/bdi>), as in aqamtu al-ʿūd fa-qāma, “I set the stick straight, so it stood upright”; or it means “carrying out God’s command.” “Through the hours of the night” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آناء الليل<\/bdi>) is an adverbial time expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظرف<\/bdi>); the singular is <i>iny<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِنْي<\/bdi>), with kasra on the hamza and an unvowelled nūn, like <i>niḥy/anḥāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نِحْي/أَنْحَاء<\/bdi>); or <i>inā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِنًى<\/bdi>), like <i>miʿā/amʿāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِعًى/أَمْعَاء<\/bdi>), according to the forms transmitted here. “You shall not be denied it” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لن تكفروه<\/bdi>) means “you shall not be deprived of it.” Kafara is here used with two objects (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعولين<\/bdi>) because it incorporates the meaning of “deprive” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حرم<\/bdi>) or “withhold” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منع<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N087\">[XIV-N087]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Upright” ( قائمة ) means straight or sound ( مستقيمة ), as in aqamtu al-ʿūd fa-qāma, “I set the stick straight, so it stood upright”; or it means “carrying out God’s command.” “Through the hours of the night” ( آناء الليل ) is an adverbial time expression ( ظرف ); the singular is iny ( إِنْي ), with kasra on the hamza and an unvowelled nūn, like niḥy/anḥāʾ ( نِحْي/أَنْحَاء ); or inā ( إِنًى ), like miʿā/amʿāʾ ( مِعًى/أَمْعَاء ), according to the forms transmitted here. “You shall not be denied it” ( لن تكفروه ) means “you shall not be deprived of it.” Kafara is here used with two objects ( مفعولين ) because it incorporates the meaning of “deprive” ( حرم ) or “withhold” ( منع ). [XIV-N087]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The worshippers among the People of the Book","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: The People of the Book “are not all alike” in unbelief and hostility. Rather, among them is “a community,” that is, a group, “upright,” practicing justice and steadfast in religion, or rising to fulfil God’s command, or standing in prayer, “reciting God’s signs” during their night vigil “through the hours of the night,” meaning its periods, “while they prostrate” in their prayers. Or the reference is to the night prayer (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صلاة العشاء<\/bdi>), which the People of the Book do not perform: it is related that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, once delayed that prayer, then came out while the people were waiting and said, “Rejoice, for there is no one among the inhabitants of earth praying at this hour other than you.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N088\">[XIV-N088]<\/a><\/p><p>He then describes them through faith: “They believe in God and the Last Day, enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and hasten to good deeds.” They are ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions who accepted Islam from among the Jews. God Most High has described them with distinctive qualities not found among the Jews: the latter deviate from the truth and are not devoted to worship; they associate partners with God and deviate concerning His attributes; they describe the Last Day with qualities other than its own; they compromise in enjoining right and forbidding wrong; and they are slow to do good—unlike the description given of those among them who embraced Islam. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: These generalized contrasts belong to the commentator’s polemic.]<\/span> “They are among the righteous”: those whose condition is sound before God, who deserve His pleasure and praise. Such is God’s practice towards His servants: when a servant draws near to Him by a handspan, He draws near by an arm’s length. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N088\">[XIV-N088]<\/a><\/p><p>Therefore He says: “Whatever good you do, you shall not be denied it.” You shall not be deprived of its reward or have its recompense refused; God will recognize it gratefully and repay you for it. Denial of recompense is called “ingratitude” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كفران<\/bdi>), just as bestowal is called “gratitude” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شكر<\/bdi>). “God knows those who are mindful of Him”: their stations in Godfearingness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقاماتهم في التقوى<\/bdi>) are not hidden from Him. This intimates that Godfearingness is the beginning of goodness and among the best of works, and that the successful before God are the people of Godfearingness. May God, through His bounty, grant us its fullest share. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: The People of the Book “are not all alike” in unbelief and hostility. Rather, among them is “a community,” that is, a group, “upright,” practicing justice and steadfast in religion, or rising to fulfil God’s command, or standing in prayer, “reciting God’s signs” during their night vigil “through the hours of the night,” meaning its periods, “while they prostrate” in their prayers. Or the reference is to the night prayer ( صلاة العشاء ), which the People of the Book do not perform: it is related that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, once delayed that prayer, then came out while the people were waiting and said, “Rejoice, for there is no one among the inhabitants of earth praying at this hour other than you.” [XIV-N088] He then describes them through faith: “They believe in God and the Last Day, enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and hasten to good deeds.” They are ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions who accepted Islam from among the Jews. God Most High has described them with distinctive qualities not found among the Jews: the latter deviate from the truth and are not devoted to worship; they associate partners with God and deviate concerning His attributes; they describe the Last Day with qualities other than its own; they compromise in enjoining right and forbidding wrong; and they are slow to do good—unlike the description given of those among them who embraced Islam. [Translator’s note: These generalized contrasts belong to the commentator’s polemic.] “They are among the righteous”: those whose condition is sound before God, who deserve His pleasure and praise. Such is God’s practice towards His servants: when a servant draws near to Him by a handspan, He draws near by an arm’s length. [XIV-N088] Therefore He says: “Whatever good you do, you shall not be denied it.” You shall not be deprived of its reward or have its recompense refused; God will recognize it gratefully and repay you for it. Denial of recompense is called “ingratitude” ( كفران ), just as bestowal is called “gratitude” ( شكر ). “God knows those who are mindful of Him”: their stations in Godfearingness ( مقاماتهم في التقوى ) are not hidden from Him. This intimates that Godfearingness is the beginning of goodness and among the best of works, and that the successful before God are the people of Godfearingness. May God, through His bounty, grant us its fullest share. Amen."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Learning for gain, teaching, action and realization","html":"<p>The people of learning are not all alike. Some make knowledge into a net with which to catch the world, selling their religion for a small worldly advantage: these are corrupt scholars and unjust judges. Others study knowledge for God and teach it for God, spending their lives teaching and recording it. Some devote their aspiration to gathering and composing it; others devote themselves to acting upon it and become worshippers and ascetics, “reciting God’s signs through the hours of the night while they prostrate.” Still others refine and realize it, then turn towards inward knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم الباطن<\/bdi>), accompany the knowers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارفين<\/bdi>) and become among those brought near (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المقربين<\/bdi>). All these “hasten to good deeds and are among the righteous.” To them it is said: “Whatever good you do shall not be denied you; God knows those who are mindful of Him.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The people of learning are not all alike. Some make knowledge into a net with which to catch the world, selling their religion for a small worldly advantage: these are corrupt scholars and unjust judges. Others study knowledge for God and teach it for God, spending their lives teaching and recording it. Some devote their aspiration to gathering and composing it; others devote themselves to acting upon it and become worshippers and ascetics, “reciting God’s signs through the hours of the night while they prostrate.” Still others refine and realize it, then turn towards inward knowledge ( علم الباطن ), accompany the knowers ( العارفين ) and become among those brought near ( المقربين ). All these “hasten to good deeds and are among the righteous.” To them it is said: “Whatever good you do shall not be denied you; God knows those who are mindful of Him.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then mentioned their opposites, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then mentioned their opposites, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4069,"arabic_characters":2640},{"id":"q3-116","q":"3:116","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:116 · Wealth, children and the loss of direct knowledge of God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 397–398","cairo":"I:397–398","source_page_first":397,"source_page_last":398,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا لَنْ تُغْنِيَ عَنْهُمْ أَمْوالُهُمْ وَلا أَوْلادُهُمْ مِنَ اللَّهِ شَيْئاً وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (116)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وجحدوا ما جاء به الرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم، (لن تُغني عنهم أموالُهم ولا أولادهم من) عذاب اللَّهِ شَيْئاً وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ أي: مُلاَزِمُوها، كَمُلاَزَمَةِ الرجل لصاحبه، هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ.\nالإشارة: إِنَّ الذين كفروا بالخصوصية عند أهل زمانهم، وفاتهم اقتباس أنوارهم، لن تُغني عنهم أموالُهم ولا أولادهم ولا علومهم مما فاتهم من معرفة الله شيئًا، ماذا وَجَدَ مَنْ فَقَدَ الله؟ وماذا فقد من وجد الله؟! قال الشاعر:\nلكل شيء إذا فارقْتَهُ عِوَضٌ ... وَلَيْسَ لله إنْ فَارقَْتَ مِنْ عِوَضِ\nولا طريق لمعرفة الحق المعرفة الخاصة- أعني معرفة العيان- إلا صحبة أهل الشهود والعيان، فكلُّ من أنكرهم كان غايته الحرمان، ولزمته البطالة والخذلان، وجَرَّب، ففي التجريب علم الحقائق، ومن حُرم صحبتهم لا ينفك عن نار القطيعة وعذاب الحجاب، وعنت الحرص والتعب، عائذاً بالله من ذلك.\nثم ضرب مثلا لأعمال الكفار، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا لَنْ تُغْنِيَ عَنْهُمْ أَمْوالُهُمْ وَلا أَوْلادُهُمْ مِنَ اللَّهِ شَيْئاً وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (116)","quran_en":"116. As for those who disbelieve, neither their wealth nor their children will avail them anything against God. They are the companions of the Fire, abiding therein.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Possessions offer no exemption from divine judgment","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who disbelieve,” denying what the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, has brought, will find that “neither their wealth nor their children will avail them anything” against God’s punishment. “They are the companions of the Fire”: they remain inseparable from it, as a man remains with his companion; “they abide therein.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N089\">[XIV-N089]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who disbelieve,” denying what the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, has brought, will find that “neither their wealth nor their children will avail them anything” against God’s punishment. “They are the companions of the Fire”: they remain inseparable from it, as a man remains with his companion; “they abide therein.” [XIV-N089]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Denying the friends of God and losing the opportunity for witnessing","html":"<p>Those who deny the distinctive spiritual realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) of the people of their time, thereby missing the opportunity to receive some of their lights, will find that neither their wealth, their children, nor even their acquired sciences can compensate for the knowledge of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة الله<\/bdi>) they have missed. What has the one who lost God found? And what has the one who found God lost? The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">For everything, once you part from it, there is a replacement;<br/>But if you part from God, there is nothing that can replace Him.<\/blockquote><p>There is no path to the special knowledge of the Real (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة الحق المعرفة الخاصة<\/bdi>)—by which I mean direct, first-hand knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة العيان<\/bdi>)—except through companionship with people of witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>). Thus whoever rejects them has deprivation as his ultimate lot, and idleness and abandonment cling to him. Let him put it to the test: experiment discloses the realities. Whoever is deprived of their companionship remains exposed to the fire of separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نار القطيعة<\/bdi>), the torment of veiling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عذاب الحجاب<\/bdi>), and the misery of grasping effort. We seek refuge in God from that.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Those who deny the distinctive spiritual realization ( الخصوصية ) of the people of their time, thereby missing the opportunity to receive some of their lights, will find that neither their wealth, their children, nor even their acquired sciences can compensate for the knowledge of God ( معرفة الله ) they have missed. What has the one who lost God found? And what has the one who found God lost? The poet says: For everything, once you part from it, there is a replacement; But if you part from God, there is nothing that can replace Him. There is no path to the special knowledge of the Real ( معرفة الحق المعرفة الخاصة )—by which I mean direct, first-hand knowledge ( معرفة العيان )—except through companionship with people of witnessing and direct vision ( أهل الشهود والعيان ). Thus whoever rejects them has deprivation as his ultimate lot, and idleness and abandonment cling to him. Let him put it to the test: experiment discloses the realities. Whoever is deprived of their companionship remains exposed to the fire of separation ( نار القطيعة ), the torment of veiling ( عذاب الحجاب ), and the misery of grasping effort. We seek refuge in God from that."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then offered a similitude for the works of the disbelievers.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then offered a similitude for the works of the disbelievers."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1612,"arabic_characters":1000},{"id":"q3-117","q":"3:117","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:117 · The wind that destroys a harvest; sincerity and the diseases of deeds","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 398–399","cairo":"I:398–399","source_page_first":398,"source_page_last":399,"ar":"مَثَلُ ما يُنْفِقُونَ فِي هذِهِ الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا كَمَثَلِ رِيحٍ فِيها صِرٌّ أَصابَتْ حَرْثَ قَوْمٍ ظَلَمُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ فَأَهْلَكَتْهُ وَما ظَلَمَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَلكِنْ أَنْفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ (117)\nقلت: في الكلام حذف، أي: مثل تلف ما ينفقون كمثل إتلاف ريح ... الخ، و (الصر) : البرد الشديد، أو ريح فيها صوت وبرد، أو السموم الحارة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: مثل ما يُنفق الكفار، قربة أو مفاخرة وسمعة، أو ما ينفق سفلة اليهود على أحبارهم، أو المنافقون رياء وخوفاً، كَمَثَلِ رِيحٍ\nفيها برد شديد أَصابَتْ حَرْثَ قَوْمٍ\nأي: زرعهم، فأتلفته وأهلكته، والمراد: تشبيه نفقتهم وأعمالهم في تلفه وضياعه وعدم الانتفاع به، بحرث كفار، ضربته ريح فيها برد فاجتاحته، فأصبح صعيداً زلقاً، ولم تبق فيه منفعة في الدنيا والآخرة، وَما ظَلَمَهُمُ اللَّهُ\nبأن ضيع أعمالهم من غير سبب، ولكنهم ظلموا أنفسهم بارتكاب الكفر الذي أحبط أعمالهم.\nالإشارة: كل مَن لم يحقق مقام الإخلاص، ولم يصحب أهل التخليص والاختصاص، لا تنفك أعماله من علل، ولا أحواله من دخل، فأعماله فارغة خفيفة، أقل ريح تقلعها وتسقطها عن درجة الاعتبار، وما زالت العامة تقول: الصحيح يصح، والخاوي يدريه الريح. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم حذَّر الحق تعالى من مخالطة أهل التخليط، فقال:","quran_ar":"مَثَلُ ما يُنْفِقُونَ فِي هذِهِ الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا كَمَثَلِ رِيحٍ فِيها صِرٌّ أَصابَتْ حَرْثَ قَوْمٍ ظَلَمُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ فَأَهْلَكَتْهُ وَما ظَلَمَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَلكِنْ أَنْفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ (117)","quran_en":"117. The likeness of what they spend in this worldly life is that of a wind bearing a bitter cold, striking the cultivation of people who wronged themselves and destroying it. God did not wrong them; rather, they wrong themselves.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"An implied verb of destruction and the meanings of ṣirr (صِرّ)","html":"<p>I say: An ellipsis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذف<\/bdi>) is present in the discourse: “The likeness of the <i>loss<\/i> of what they spend is like the <i>destruction<\/i> brought about by a wind,” and so forth. The <strong>ṣirr<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصِّرّ<\/bdi>) is piercing cold. Or the phrase signifies a wind containing a sound and cold; or a scorching, poisonous hot wind (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السموم الحارة<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N090\">[XIV-N090]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: An ellipsis ( حذف ) is present in the discourse: “The likeness of the loss of what they spend is like the destruction brought about by a wind,” and so forth. The ṣirr ( الصِّرّ ) is piercing cold. Or the phrase signifies a wind containing a sound and cold; or a scorching, poisonous hot wind ( السموم الحارة ). [XIV-N090]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The expenditure that yields no lasting benefit","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: What the unbelievers expend, whether as a devotional offering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قربة<\/bdi>) or for boasting and reputation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفاخرة وسمعة<\/bdi>); or what the lowly among the Jews expend upon their learned authorities; or what the hypocrites expend ostentatiously (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رياء<\/bdi>) or out of fear—is like a wind containing severe cold that strikes the cultivated land (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حَرْث<\/bdi>) of some people, that is, their growing crops, and ruins and destroys them. The intended comparison is between their expenditure and deeds, in their perishing, being wasted and bringing them no benefit, and the cultivation of unbelievers struck by a freezing wind that lays it waste. It becomes a smooth, bare expanse, yielding no benefit in this world or the next. God did not wrong them by letting their works go to waste without a cause: they wronged themselves by committing the unbelief that made their works fruitless. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N091\">[XIV-N091]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: What the unbelievers expend, whether as a devotional offering ( قربة ) or for boasting and reputation ( مفاخرة وسمعة ); or what the lowly among the Jews expend upon their learned authorities; or what the hypocrites expend ostentatiously ( رياء ) or out of fear—is like a wind containing severe cold that strikes the cultivated land ( حَرْث ) of some people, that is, their growing crops, and ruins and destroys them. The intended comparison is between their expenditure and deeds, in their perishing, being wasted and bringing them no benefit, and the cultivation of unbelievers struck by a freezing wind that lays it waste. It becomes a smooth, bare expanse, yielding no benefit in this world or the next. God did not wrong them by letting their works go to waste without a cause: they wronged themselves by committing the unbelief that made their works fruitless. [XIV-N091]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Acts unpurified by sincere intention","html":"<p>Whoever has not realized the station of sincerity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإخلاص<\/bdi>) and has not accompanied the people of purification and special spiritual realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التخليص والاختصاص<\/bdi>) cannot keep his works free of hidden defects (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علل<\/bdi>), nor his spiritual states free of adulteration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دخل<\/bdi>). His works are empty and light; the slightest wind uproots them and casts them down from the rank of being taken into account. Ordinary people have long said: “What is sound stays sound; what is hollow the wind carries away.” And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever has not realized the station of sincerity ( مقام الإخلاص ) and has not accompanied the people of purification and special spiritual realization ( أهل التخليص والاختصاص ) cannot keep his works free of hidden defects ( علل ), nor his spiritual states free of adulteration ( دخل ). His works are empty and light; the slightest wind uproots them and casts them down from the rank of being taken into account. Ordinary people have long said: “What is sound stays sound; what is hollow the wind carries away.” And success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then warned against association with people who bring about confusion and mixture.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then warned against association with people who bring about confusion and mixture."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1874,"arabic_characters":1098},{"id":"q3-118-120","q":"3:118–120","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:118–120 · Intimate confidants, the concealment of enmity and the safeguarding of spiritual secrets","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 399–401","cairo":"I:399–401","source_page_first":399,"source_page_last":401,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَتَّخِذُوا بِطانَةً مِنْ دُونِكُمْ لا يَأْلُونَكُمْ خَبالاً وَدُّوا ما عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضاءُ مِنْ أَفْواهِهِمْ وَما تُخْفِي صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ قَدْ بَيَّنَّا لَكُمُ الْآياتِ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (118) ها أَنْتُمْ أُولاءِ تُحِبُّونَهُمْ وَلا يُحِبُّونَكُمْ وَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِالْكِتابِ كُلِّهِ وَإِذا لَقُوكُمْ قالُوا آمَنَّا وَإِذا خَلَوْا عَضُّوا عَلَيْكُمُ الْأَنامِلَ مِنَ الْغَيْظِ قُلْ مُوتُوا بِغَيْظِكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ (119) إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ تَسُؤْهُمْ وَإِنْ تُصِبْكُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ يَفْرَحُوا بِها وَإِنْ تَصْبِرُوا وَتَتَّقُوا لا يَضُرُّكُمْ كَيْدُهُمْ شَيْئاً إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطٌ (120)\nقلت: بطانة الرجل: خواصه الذين يطلعهم على باطنه وسره، وسميت بطانة تشبيهاً لها بالثوب الذي يلي بطنه كالشعار. قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «الأَنْصَارَ شِعَارٌ والنَّاسُ دِثَارٌ» . وهي اسم تطلق على المفرد والجمع والمذكر والمؤنث. والألو: التقصير، وأصله: أن يتعدى بالحرف، تقول: لا آلو في نصحك أي: لا أقصر فيه. ثم عدي إلى مفعولين، كقولهم: لا آلوك نصحاً، على تضمن معنى المنع أو النقص. والخبال: الفساد.\nو (ما عنتم) : مصدرية، والعنت: التعب والمشقة، والأنامل: جمع أنملة- بضم الميم وفتحها-، والضير والضر واحد. ومضارع الأول: يضير، والثاني: يضر، وهو هنا مجزوم، وأصله: يضرُركم، نقلت حركة الراء إلى الضاد، وضمت الراء، إتباعاً لحركة الضاد طلباً للمشاكلة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَتَّخِذُوا بِطانَةً أي: أصدقاء وأصفياء، تطلعونهم على سركم، وهم مِنْ دُونِكُمْ ليسوا على دينكم، فإنهم لا يَأْلُونَكُمْ خَبالًا أي: لا يقصرون جهدَهم في إدخال الفساد بينكم بالتخليط والنميمة وإطلاع الكفار على عورتكم. نزلت في رجال من المسلمين، كانوا يصلون رجالاً من اليهود لما كان بينهم من القرابة والصداقة، أو في المنافقين كان يصلهم بعض المسلمين.\nثم وصفهم بأوصاف توجب التنفير منهم فقال: وَدُّوا ما عَنِتُّمْ أي: تمنوا عنتكم وهلاككم وضلالكم، قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضاءُ مِنْ أَفْواهِهِمْ أي: ظهرت أمارة العداوة من أفواههم بالوقيعة في المسلمين، أو بإطلاع المشركين على عوراتهم، أو في كلامهم مع المسلمين بالغيظ، لأنهم لا يتمالكون أنفسهم لفرط بغضهم، وَما تُخْفِي\nصُدُورُهُمْ\nمن العداوة والبغضاء، أَكْبَرُ مما أظهروه، لأن ظهوره منهم ليس عن روية واختيار، بل عن غلبة غيظ واضطرار. قَدْ بَيَّنَّا لَكُمُ أيها المؤمنون الْآياتِ الدالة على مجانبة الكافرين وموالاة المؤمنين، إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ ما يبين لكم.\nها أَنْتُمْ يا هؤلاء المخاطبين تُحِبُّونَهُمْ لما بينكم من المصاهرة والصداقة، وَلا يُحِبُّونَكُمْ لما بينكم من مخالفة الدين، أو تريدون لهم الإسلام وهم يريدون لكم الكفر، وأنتم تُؤْمِنُونَ بِالْكِتابِ أي: بجنس الكتب، (كله) أي: بالكتب كلها، وهم لا يؤمنون بكتابكم، فكيف تحبونهم وهم يكذبون كتابكم ورسولكم؟ وهم أيضاً ينافقونكم إِذا لَقُوكُمْ قالُوا آمَنَّا وَإِذا خَلَوْا مع أنفسهم عَضُّوا عَلَيْكُمُ الْأَنامِلَ مِنَ الْغَيْظِ لما يرون من ائتلاف المؤمنين، ولم يجدوا سبيلاً إلى التشفي فيكم، وهذه كناية عن شدة حقدهم، وإن لم يكن ثَمَّ عض في الخارج.\nقال لهم الحق جل جلاله: قُلْ لهم يا محمد: مُوتُوا بِغَيْظِكُمْ فإنما ضرر غيظكم عليكم، أو دوموا على غيظكم حتى تموتوا عليه، فإن مادة الإسلام لا تزال تنمو حتى تهلكوا، إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ أي:\nبحقيقة ما في قلوبكم من البغضاء والحَنَقَ(1) ، أو بما في القلوب من خير أو شر. هو من مقول الرسول لهم، أو من كلام الله تعالى، استئناف، أي: لا تعجب من إطلاعي إياك على أسرارهم، فإني عليم بالأخفى من ضمائرهم.\nومن فرط عداوتهم أنهم إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ كنصر وغنيمة تَسُؤْهُمْ أي: تحزنهم، وَإِنْ تُصِبْكُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ كهزيمة أو قتل أو إصابة عدو منكم او اختلاف بينكم، يَفْرَحُوا بِها، وَإِنْ تَصْبِرُوا على عداوتهم وأذاهم، وتخافوا ربكم، وَتَتَّقُوا ما نهاكم عنه، لا يَضُرُّكُمْ كَيْدُهُمْ شَيْئاً، بفضل الله وحفظه، الموعود للصابرين والمتقين، إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ، إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا. ومن كان الحق معه لا يضره شيء، إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطٌ لا يخفى عليه ما يعمل أهل الكفر من العداوة والحقد، فيجازيهم عليه.\nالإشارة: لا ينبغي لأهل الخصوصية أن يتخذوا بطانة من دونهم من العامة حتى يطلعوهم على سرهم، فإن الإطلاع على السر، ولو كان غير الخصوصية، كله ضعف في العقل ووهن في الرأي، وفي ذلك يقول القائل:\n(من أطْلَعَ الناسَ على سره ... استحقَّ الكيَّ على جَبْهَتِه)\nوأما سر الربوبية فإفشاؤه لغير أهله حرام، والعامة مضادون لأهل الخصوصية، لا يألونهم خبالاً في قلوبهم وتشتيتاً لفكرتهم، إذا صحبوهم يودون أن لو كانوا مثلهم في العنت وتعب الأسباب، فإذا ظهر بالفقراء نقص أو خلل\nظهرت البغضاء من أفواههم، وما تخفي صدورهم أكبر، فإن كنتم أيها الفقراء تحبون لهم الخير فإنهم بعكس ذلك، وإن كنتم تقرون شريعتهم فإنهم لا يؤمنون بحقيقتكم، بل ينكرونها عليكم، ومنهم من يتصف بالنفاق، إذا لقي أهل الخصوصية أظهر التصديق والمحبة، وإذا خلا مع العامة أظهر العداوة والحَنَق، وإن تمسسكم أيها الفقراء حسنة، كعز وفتح وشهود ومعرفة تَسؤْهم، وإن تصبكم سيئة كمحنة أو بلية، يفرحوا بها، وإن تصبروا على أذاهم وجفوتهم، وتتقوا شهود السوى فيهم، لا يضركم كيدهم شيئاً (إن الله بما يعملون محيط) .\nولما فرغ الحق تعالى من معاتبة أهل الكتابين، شرع فى معاتبة بعض المسلمين لما وقع لهم في غزوة أحد من الفشل، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَتَّخِذُوا بِطانَةً مِنْ دُونِكُمْ لا يَأْلُونَكُمْ خَبالاً وَدُّوا ما عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضاءُ مِنْ أَفْواهِهِمْ وَما تُخْفِي صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ قَدْ بَيَّنَّا لَكُمُ الْآياتِ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (118) ها أَنْتُمْ أُولاءِ تُحِبُّونَهُمْ وَلا يُحِبُّونَكُمْ وَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِالْكِتابِ كُلِّهِ وَإِذا لَقُوكُمْ قالُوا آمَنَّا وَإِذا خَلَوْا عَضُّوا عَلَيْكُمُ الْأَنامِلَ مِنَ الْغَيْظِ قُلْ مُوتُوا بِغَيْظِكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ (119) إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ تَسُؤْهُمْ وَإِنْ تُصِبْكُمْ سَيِّئَةٌ يَفْرَحُوا بِها وَإِنْ تَصْبِرُوا وَتَتَّقُوا لا يَضُرُّكُمْ كَيْدُهُمْ شَيْئاً إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطٌ (120)","quran_en":"118. O you who believe, do not take as intimate confidants those outside yourselves, who will spare no effort to corrupt your affairs. They wish you hardship. Hatred has already appeared from their mouths, and what their breasts conceal is greater. We have made the signs clear to you, if you use reason. 119. Here you are: you love them, whereas they do not love you, and you believe in the entire Book. When they meet you, they say, “We believe”; but when they are alone, they bite their fingertips in rage at you. Say: “Die in your rage!” God knows what lies within breasts. 120. If something good touches you, it grieves them; if something bad befalls you, they rejoice in it. But if you are patient and mindful of God, their plotting will not harm you in the least. God encompasses all that they do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Biṭāna (بِطانة), alw (أَلْو), ʿanat (عَنَت), and the jussive lā yaḍurrukum","html":"<p>I say: A man’s <strong>biṭāna<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بطانة<\/bdi>) comprises his intimates, whom he allows to see his inner affairs and secrets. The word is derived by comparison with the garment next to his belly—the inner garment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شِعار<\/bdi>). The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, as quoted here: “The Helpers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأنصار<\/bdi>) are the close garment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شعار<\/bdi>), whereas other people are the outer covering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دثار<\/bdi>).” This noun can designate an individual or a group, masculine or feminine alike.<\/p><p><strong>Alw<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الألو<\/bdi>) means failing or falling short. Ordinarily the verb is construed with a preposition: “I do not fall short <i>in<\/i> counselling you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا آلو في نصحك<\/bdi>). Here it is made to govern two objects, as in “I will not deprive you of counsel” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا آلوك نصحاً<\/bdi>), through its implication of preventing or diminishing. <strong>Khabāl<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخبال<\/bdi>) is corruption or ruin.<\/p><p>In “what brings you hardship” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما عنتم<\/bdi>), <strong>mā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما<\/bdi>) is a particle converting the clause to a verbal-noun sense (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية<\/bdi>); <strong>ʿanat<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العنت<\/bdi>) signifies fatigue and hardship. <strong>Anāmil<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأنامل<\/bdi>) is the plural of <strong>anmula<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنملة<\/bdi>), pronounced with either ḍamma or fatḥa on the mīm. <strong>Ḍayr<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الضير<\/bdi>) and <strong>ḍurr<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الضر<\/bdi>) mean much the same: their imperfect verbs are respectively <strong>yaḍīru<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يضير<\/bdi>) and <strong>yaḍurru<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يضر<\/bdi>). The latter occurs here in the jussive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجزوم<\/bdi>). Its underlying form is <strong>yaḍrurkum<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يضرُركم<\/bdi>); the vowel of the first rāʾ is transferred to the ḍād and the remaining rāʾ is given ḍamma to agree with the ḍād's vowel, producing a phonetic harmony (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشاكلة<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N092\">[XIV-N092]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: A man’s biṭāna ( بطانة ) comprises his intimates, whom he allows to see his inner affairs and secrets. The word is derived by comparison with the garment next to his belly—the inner garment ( شِعار ). The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, as quoted here: “The Helpers ( الأنصار ) are the close garment ( شعار ), whereas other people are the outer covering ( دثار ).” This noun can designate an individual or a group, masculine or feminine alike. Alw ( الألو ) means failing or falling short. Ordinarily the verb is construed with a preposition: “I do not fall short in counselling you” ( لا آلو في نصحك ). Here it is made to govern two objects, as in “I will not deprive you of counsel” ( لا آلوك نصحاً ), through its implication of preventing or diminishing. Khabāl ( الخبال ) is corruption or ruin. In “what brings you hardship” ( ما عنتم ), mā ( ما ) is a particle converting the clause to a verbal-noun sense ( مصدرية ); ʿanat ( العنت ) signifies fatigue and hardship. Anāmil ( الأنامل ) is the plural of anmula ( أنملة ), pronounced with either ḍamma or fatḥa on the mīm. Ḍayr ( الضير ) and ḍurr ( الضر ) mean much the same: their imperfect verbs are respectively yaḍīru ( يضير ) and yaḍurru ( يضر ). The latter occurs here in the jussive ( مجزوم ). Its underlying form is yaḍrurkum ( يضرُركم ); the vowel of the first rāʾ is transferred to the ḍād and the remaining rāʾ is given ḍamma to agree with the ḍād's vowel, producing a phonetic harmony ( مشاكلة ). [XIV-N092]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The reported setting and exposure of concealed resentment","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, do not take intimate confidants (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بطانة<\/bdi>)”—friends and chosen intimates to whom you disclose your secrets—from people outside your religious community, since “they will not spare any effort to corrupt your affairs,” striving to introduce disorder through confusion and malicious tales, and revealing your vulnerable places to unbelievers. According to one report on the occasion of revelation, it concerned Muslims who maintained close ties with certain Jewish men through kinship and friendship. According to another, it concerned hypocrites whom some Muslims associated with closely. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N093\">[XIV-N093]<\/a><\/p><p>He then describes them with qualities designed to repel such intimacy: “They wish you hardship,” meaning your suffering, ruin and misguidance. “Hatred has already appeared from their mouths”: signs of hostility have surfaced through attacks on Muslims, revelation of their vulnerabilities to the idolaters, or words of rage addressed to Muslims. The force of their hatred leaves them unable to contain themselves. “What their breasts conceal” of hostility and rancour “is greater” than what has slipped into public expression, because their spoken displays spring not from careful deliberation or free choice, but from overpowering rage. “We have made the signs clear to you,” O believers—those indicating the need to avoid unbelievers and be loyal to the faithful—“if you understand” what has been set before you.<\/p><p>“Here you are,” those being addressed: “You love them” because of marriage connections or friendship, “and they do not love you” because of your religious differences. Alternatively, you desire Islam for them, while they desire disbelief for you. You “believe in the Book,” that is, in the category of revealed books—and “all of it,” meaning all the revealed books—while they do not believe in your Book. How, Ibn ʿAjība asks, can you love those who reject your Book and Messenger? They also dissemble in your presence: “When they meet you they say, ‘We believe,’” but “when they are alone” they “bite their fingertips in rage” at the concord they see among believers and their own inability to gain satisfaction against you. This is a figurative expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كناية<\/bdi>) for the intensity of their resentment, even if no physical biting actually occurs.<\/p><p>The Real says to the Prophet, “Say to them, ‘Die in your rage!’” That rage can injure only yourselves—or remain in it until you die, for the strength of Islam will continue to grow until you perish. “God knows what lies in breasts”: the reality of the animosity and wrath in their hearts, or all the good and evil that hearts contain. This may be part of what the Messenger says to them; alternatively it may be God’s own renewed statement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استئناف<\/bdi>): Do not be astonished that I disclosed their secrets to you, for I know even what lies more deeply hidden within them.<\/p><p>In the excess of their hostility, “If good touches you,” such as victory or spoils, it saddens them. “If something bad befalls you,” such as defeat, death, an enemy’s attack or dissension among you, they rejoice. “But if you are patient” in the face of their hostility and injury, “and are mindful of God” and abstain from what He has forbidden, “their plot will not harm you at all,” through God’s gracious protection promised to those who patiently persevere and remain mindful. “God is with the patient”; and “God is with those who are mindful.” Whoever has the Real with him cannot be harmed by anything. “God encompasses what they do”: the deeds of the unbelievers, their hatred and hostility, do not escape His knowledge, and He will recompense them accordingly.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, do not take intimate confidants ( بطانة )”—friends and chosen intimates to whom you disclose your secrets—from people outside your religious community, since “they will not spare any effort to corrupt your affairs,” striving to introduce disorder through confusion and malicious tales, and revealing your vulnerable places to unbelievers. According to one report on the occasion of revelation, it concerned Muslims who maintained close ties with certain Jewish men through kinship and friendship. According to another, it concerned hypocrites whom some Muslims associated with closely. [XIV-N093] He then describes them with qualities designed to repel such intimacy: “They wish you hardship,” meaning your suffering, ruin and misguidance. “Hatred has already appeared from their mouths”: signs of hostility have surfaced through attacks on Muslims, revelation of their vulnerabilities to the idolaters, or words of rage addressed to Muslims. The force of their hatred leaves them unable to contain themselves. “What their breasts conceal” of hostility and rancour “is greater” than what has slipped into public expression, because their spoken displays spring not from careful deliberation or free choice, but from overpowering rage. “We have made the signs clear to you,” O believers—those indicating the need to avoid unbelievers and be loyal to the faithful—“if you understand” what has been set before you. “Here you are,” those being addressed: “You love them” because of marriage connections or friendship, “and they do not love you” because of your religious differences. Alternatively, you desire Islam for them, while they desire disbelief for you. You “believe in the Book,” that is, in the category of revealed books—and “all of it,” meaning all the revealed books—while they do not believe in your Book. How, Ibn ʿAjība asks, can you love those who reject your Book and Messenger? They also dissemble in your presence: “When they meet you they say, ‘We believe,’” but “when they are alone” they “bite their fingertips in rage” at the concord they see among believers and their own inability to gain satisfaction against you. This is a figurative expression ( كناية ) for the intensity of their resentment, even if no physical biting actually occurs. The Real says to the Prophet, “Say to them, ‘Die in your rage!’” That rage can injure only yourselves—or remain in it until you die, for the strength of Islam will continue to grow until you perish. “God knows what lies in breasts”: the reality of the animosity and wrath in their hearts, or all the good and evil that hearts contain. This may be part of what the Messenger says to them; alternatively it may be God’s own renewed statement ( استئناف ): Do not be astonished that I disclosed their secrets to you, for I know even what lies more deeply hidden within them. In the excess of their hostility, “If good touches you,” such as victory or spoils, it saddens them. “If something bad befalls you,” such as defeat, death, an enemy’s attack or dissension among you, they rejoice. “But if you are patient” in the face of their hostility and injury, “and are mindful of God” and abstain from what He has forbidden, “their plot will not harm you at all,” through God’s gracious protection promised to those who patiently persevere and remain mindful. “God is with the patient”; and “God is with those who are mindful.” Whoever has the Real with him cannot be harmed by anything. “God encompasses what they do”: the deeds of the unbelievers, their hatred and hostility, do not escape His knowledge, and He will recompense them accordingly."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The discipline of confidence among the spiritually realized","html":"<p>It does not befit the people of special realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الخصوصية<\/bdi>) to choose intimates outside their company from the common people (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العامة<\/bdi>) and unveil their secret (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سرّ<\/bdi>) to them. Indeed, divulging secrets, even when they are not secrets of special realization, betrays a weak intellect and poor judgment. Hence the saying:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Whoever makes his private secret known to people<br/>Deserves to be branded upon the forehead.<\/blockquote><p>To reveal the secret of Lordship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سر الربوبية<\/bdi>) to those unfit for it, however, is forbidden. The common people stand opposed, in Ibn ʿAjība’s spiritual analogy, to the people of special realization: they do not cease to sow corruption in their hearts and disperse their focused reflection. If they accompany them, they wish that the Sufis might live as they do, in hardship and the toil of secondary causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعب الأسباب<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>If some deficiency or flaw appears among the spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>), enmity appears in the critics’ speech, and the feelings they conceal are worse. You, spiritual poor, desire good for them, yet they desire the opposite for you. You acknowledge their adherence to the revealed Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شريعة<\/bdi>), but they do not acknowledge your spiritual reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حقيقة<\/bdi>); instead they deny it to you. Some behave hypocritically: when they meet the people of special realization they profess belief and affection, but when alone with the crowd they manifest hostility and resentment.<\/p><p>If good touches you, O spiritual poor—honour, an opening (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فتح<\/bdi>), witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود<\/bdi>) or knowledge of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة<\/bdi>)—it distresses them; if an ordeal or calamity befalls you, they rejoice. But if you patiently endure their harshness and estrangement, and guard against the perception of anything-other-than-God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود السوى<\/bdi>) in them, their plotting will not harm you. “God encompasses all that they do.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N094\">[XIV-N094]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"It does not befit the people of special realization ( أهل الخصوصية ) to choose intimates outside their company from the common people ( العامة ) and unveil their secret ( سرّ ) to them. Indeed, divulging secrets, even when they are not secrets of special realization, betrays a weak intellect and poor judgment. Hence the saying: Whoever makes his private secret known to people Deserves to be branded upon the forehead. To reveal the secret of Lordship ( سر الربوبية ) to those unfit for it, however, is forbidden. The common people stand opposed, in Ibn ʿAjība’s spiritual analogy, to the people of special realization: they do not cease to sow corruption in their hearts and disperse their focused reflection. If they accompany them, they wish that the Sufis might live as they do, in hardship and the toil of secondary causes ( تعب الأسباب ). If some deficiency or flaw appears among the spiritual poor ( الفقراء ), enmity appears in the critics’ speech, and the feelings they conceal are worse. You, spiritual poor, desire good for them, yet they desire the opposite for you. You acknowledge their adherence to the revealed Law ( شريعة ), but they do not acknowledge your spiritual reality ( حقيقة ); instead they deny it to you. Some behave hypocritically: when they meet the people of special realization they profess belief and affection, but when alone with the crowd they manifest hostility and resentment. If good touches you, O spiritual poor—honour, an opening ( فتح ), witnessing ( شهود ) or knowledge of God ( معرفة )—it distresses them; if an ordeal or calamity befalls you, they rejoice. But if you patiently endure their harshness and estrangement, and guard against the perception of anything-other-than-God ( شهود السوى ) in them, their plotting will not harm you. “God encompasses all that they do.” [XIV-N094]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having concluded His reproach of the two People of the Book, the Real begins to rebuke some Muslims for the faltering that occurred at the Battle of Uḥud.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having concluded His reproach of the two People of the Book, the Real begins to rebuke some Muslims for the faltering that occurred at the Battle of Uḥud."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C400.1","page":400,"ar":"الحنق: شدة الاغتياظ.","en":"Ḥanaq: intense rage.","anchor":"والحَنَقَ"}],"english_characters":7141,"arabic_characters":4818},{"id":"q3-121-122","q":"3:121–122","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:121–122 · Uḥud: consultation, steadfastness and the guides who prepare the soul for struggle","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 401–402","cairo":"I:401–402","source_page_first":401,"source_page_last":402,"ar":"وَإِذْ غَدَوْتَ مِنْ أَهْلِكَ تُبَوِّئُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ مَقاعِدَ لِلْقِتالِ وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (121) إِذْ هَمَّتْ طائِفَتانِ مِنْكُمْ أَنْ تَفْشَلا وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّهُما وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ (122)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكر يا محمد حين غَدَوْتَ مِنْ أَهْلِكَ من منزل عائشة، الذي نَزَلْتَ فيه بأحد، حين خرجت بها، حال كونك تُبَوِّئُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أي: تهيئ لهم، مَقاعِدَ لِلْقِتالِ أي: مواقف وأماكن يقفون فيها للحرب وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ لأقوالكم، عَلِيمٌ بإخلاصكم.\nقال الواقدي: خرج النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم من منزل عائشة- رضي الله عنها- ماشياً على رجليه إلى أُحد، فجعل يصف أصحابه للقتال كأنما يقوم بهم القدح(1) . إن رأى صدراً خارجاً، قال: تأخر. وذلك أن المشركين نزلوا بأحد، يوم الأربعاء، فلما سمع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بنزولهم استشار أصحابه، ودعا عبد الله بن أبي بن سلول- ولم يدْعُه قط قبلها- فاستشاره، فقال عبد الله بن أبي وأكثر الأنصار: يا رسول الله أَقِمْ بالمدينة ولا تخرج إليهم، فو الله ما خرجنا منها إلى عدو قط إلا أصاب منا، ولا دخل علينا إلا أصبنا منه، فكيف وأنت فينا! فدعهم يا رسول الله، فإن أقاموا أقاموا بشر محبس، وإن دخلوا قاتلهم الرجال في وجوههم، ورماهم النساء والصبيان بالحجارة، وإن رجعوا رجعوا خاسئين. فأعجب النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم هذا الرأي، وقال بعض أصحابه: يا رسول الله اخرج بنا إلى هذه الأكْلُب(2) لا يرون أنا جَبُنَّا عنهم وضعفنا. فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «إِنِّي رأيتُ في منامِي بَقَراً تذبح، فأولتها ناساً من أصحابي يُقتلون، ورأيت في ذُبابِ سَيْفي ثلماً(3) ، فأولتها هزيمةً، ورأيت أني أدخل يدي في درع حصينة، فأولتها المدينة، فإن رأيتُمْ أن تُقيموا بالمدينة وتدعوهم فافعلوا» . فقال رجال ممن فاتهم بدر، وأكرمهم الله بالشهادة يوم أحد: اخرج بنا\nإلى أعدائنا، وبالغوا، حتى دخل النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم ولبس لأمته(1) . فلما رأوه قد لبس سلاحه ندموا، وقالوا: بئس ما صنعنا، نشير على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم والوحي يأتيه، فقاموا واعتذروا إليه. وقالوا: اصنع ما رأيت، فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لا يَنْبَغِي لنَبِي أَنْ يلبَس لأمَتَهُ فيَضَعَهَا حتَى يُقاتِلَ» .\nفخرج بعد صلاة الجمعة، وأصبح بشعب من أُحُد، يوم السبت للنصف من شوال، سنة ثلاث من الهجرة، ونزل في عدوة من الوادي، وجعل ظهره وعسكره إلى أحد، وسوى صفهم كما تقدم، وأمر عبد الله بن جبير على الرماة، وقال: انضحوا عنا بالنبل، لا يأتونا من خلفنا، فكان من أمر الله ما كان، على ما يأتي(2) .\nوخرج مع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم في غزوة أحد زهاء ألف، ووعدهم النصر إن صبروا، فلما بلغوا الشواط- موضع- انخزل ابنُ أُبيّ في ثلاثمائة، وقال: علام نقتل أنفسنا! فتبعهم أبو جابر السلمي، فقال: أُنشدكم الله في نبيكم وأنفسكم. فقال ابن أُبيّ: لو نعلم قتالاً لاتبعناكم، وهمت بنو حارثة وبنو سلمة بالانصراف معه، فثبتوا مع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فذكرهم نعمته بقوله: إِذْ هَمَّتْ طائِفَتانِ مِنْكُمْ أَنْ تَفْشَلا وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّهُما وناصرهما، حيث عصمهما من اتباع المنافقين، قال جابر: (ما يسرنا أنها لم تنزل، لقوله: وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّهُما) فبنو سلمة من الخزرج، وبنو حارثة من الأوس، وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ لا على غيره، إذ لا ناصر غيره.\nالإشارة: من شأن شيوخ التربية أن يَدُلُّوا المريدين على محاربة النفوس ومقاتلتها، ويطلعوهم على دسائسها ومخادعتها، ليهيئوا لهم بذلك مقاعد لقتالها، والله مطلع على إخلاصهم ونياتهم، فمنهم من يمل ويكل، فيرجع إلى وطن عوائده، ومنهم من يصبر حتى يفوز بالغنيمة العظمى والسعادة القصوى، وفي ذلك يقول القائل:\nوبَالَغَوا في الجدِّ حتى مَلَّ أكثرهُم ... وعَانَقَ المجْدَ مَنْ وَافَى ومَنْ صَبَرَا\nقال بعضهم: انتهى سير السائرين إلى الظفر بنفوسهم، فإن ظفروا بها وصلوا. هـ. ومنهم من يلحقه الملل والفشل فيهم بالانصراف والرجوع، ثم يثبته الله تعالى وينصره، فيلحق بالصابرين السابقين، وعمدة المريد في مجاهدة نفسه: التوكل على الله والاعتماد عليه دون شيء سواه «من علامة النجح في النهايات: الرجوع إلى الله في البدايات» . وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ.\nثم ذكّر أهل أحد بما وقع لهم يوم بدر من النصر والظفر مع قلتهم ليثبتوا، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ غَدَوْتَ مِنْ أَهْلِكَ تُبَوِّئُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ مَقاعِدَ لِلْقِتالِ وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (121) إِذْ هَمَّتْ طائِفَتانِ مِنْكُمْ أَنْ تَفْشَلا وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّهُما وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ (122)","quran_en":"121. And [remember] when you went forth early from your household, assigning the believers positions for battle. God is Hearing, Knowing. 122. When two groups among you almost lost courage—though God was their Protector—and in God let the believers put their trust.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The dispositions at the beginning of Uḥud","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Remember, O Muḥammad, when you went forth early from your household—from ʿĀʾisha’s lodging, where you were staying at Uḥud, having gone there with her—while you were “assigning the believers positions for battle,” arranging the positions where they would stand and fight. “God is Hearing” of your utterances and “Knowing” of your sincerity.<\/p><p>Al-Wāqidī relates that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, walked from ʿĀʾisha’s lodging to Uḥud and arranged the Companions for fighting as carefully as one straightens an arrow-shaft. When he saw a chest projecting out beyond the line, he said, “Step back.” The idolaters had encamped at Uḥud on Wednesday. Upon learning of their arrival, the Prophet consulted his Companions, and invited ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl for consultation—something he had not done before.<\/p><p>Ibn Ubayy and most of the Helpers said: “Messenger of God, remain in Madīna; do not march out to them. By God, whenever we have gone out from it to meet an enemy, that enemy has inflicted losses on us; and whenever an enemy has entered it, we have inflicted losses on him. How much more so with you among us? Leave them! If they stay, they stay in a very bad place of confinement; if they enter, our men will fight them face-to-face, while our women and children cast stones at them; and if they retreat, they will go back humbled.” The Prophet approved this judgment. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N095\">[XIV-N095]<\/a><\/p><p>Other Companions urged him: “Messenger of God, lead us out against those dogs, so that they do not think we have proved cowardly or weak before them.” The Prophet replied: “I saw in my dream cattle being slaughtered, and interpreted them as men from my Companions who would be killed. I saw a chip at the point of my sword, and interpreted it as defeat. I saw myself placing my hand inside a strong coat of mail, and interpreted it as Madīna. If you think it best to remain in Madīna and leave the enemy where they are, do so.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N096\">[XIV-N096]<\/a><\/p><p>Some men who had missed Badr, whom God would honour with martyrdom at Uḥud, insisted upon marching out against the enemy. They pressed the case until the Prophet entered and put on his coat of armour (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لأمة<\/bdi>). When they saw him armed they regretted their insistence, saying: “We did badly in advising the Prophet when revelation comes to him.” They stood and apologised: “Do whatever seems best to you.” The Prophet replied: “A prophet should not put on his coat of mail and then take it off until he has fought.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N096\">[XIV-N096]<\/a><\/p><p>He departed after the Friday prayer and was at the ravine of Uḥud by Saturday, the middle of Shawwāl, in the third year after the Hijra. He took position on one side of the valley, with Uḥud behind himself and his camp, and straightened the ranks as already described. He appointed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr to command the archers, instructing them: “Drive them off from us with arrows; do not let them reach us from behind.” Then what God decreed came to pass, as will be related subsequently.<\/p><p>About one thousand men marched out with the Prophet for Uḥud. He had promised victory if they persevered. Upon reaching al-Shawṭ, a certain location <span class=\"translator-note\">[<strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> The Cairo print spells the place-name الشواط (al-Shawāṭ). The reading al-Shawṭ (الشوط) used here is an explicit correction, supported by Ibn al-Mulaqqin’s <i>al-Tawḍīḥ<\/i>, XXI, p. 145, in its account of Uḥud.]<\/span>, Ibn Ubayy withdrew with three hundred, saying: “Why should we get ourselves killed?” Abū Jābir al-Sulamī pursued the men, imploring them by God for the sake of their Prophet and themselves. Ibn Ubayy answered, “Had we known there would be a battle, we would have followed you.” The clans of Banū Ḥāritha and Banū Salama were on the verge of turning back with him, but remained firm beside the Prophet. Thus God reminded them of His grace in the verse: “When two groups among you almost lost courage, though God was their Protector”—He supported and protected the two from following the hypocrites.<\/p><p>Jābir said: “We would not be pleased if the verse had not been revealed, because of the words ‘God was their Protector.’” Banū Salama belonged to the Khazraj and Banū Ḥāritha to the Aws. “In God let the believers put their trust,” and not in anything else, for there is no other true Helper. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N097\">[XIV-N097]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Remember, O Muḥammad, when you went forth early from your household—from ʿĀʾisha’s lodging, where you were staying at Uḥud, having gone there with her—while you were “assigning the believers positions for battle,” arranging the positions where they would stand and fight. “God is Hearing” of your utterances and “Knowing” of your sincerity. Al-Wāqidī relates that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, walked from ʿĀʾisha’s lodging to Uḥud and arranged the Companions for fighting as carefully as one straightens an arrow-shaft. When he saw a chest projecting out beyond the line, he said, “Step back.” The idolaters had encamped at Uḥud on Wednesday. Upon learning of their arrival, the Prophet consulted his Companions, and invited ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl for consultation—something he had not done before. Ibn Ubayy and most of the Helpers said: “Messenger of God, remain in Madīna; do not march out to them. By God, whenever we have gone out from it to meet an enemy, that enemy has inflicted losses on us; and whenever an enemy has entered it, we have inflicted losses on him. How much more so with you among us? Leave them! If they stay, they stay in a very bad place of confinement; if they enter, our men will fight them face-to-face, while our women and children cast stones at them; and if they retreat, they will go back humbled.” The Prophet approved this judgment. [XIV-N095] Other Companions urged him: “Messenger of God, lead us out against those dogs, so that they do not think we have proved cowardly or weak before them.” The Prophet replied: “I saw in my dream cattle being slaughtered, and interpreted them as men from my Companions who would be killed. I saw a chip at the point of my sword, and interpreted it as defeat. I saw myself placing my hand inside a strong coat of mail, and interpreted it as Madīna. If you think it best to remain in Madīna and leave the enemy where they are, do so.” [XIV-N096] Some men who had missed Badr, whom God would honour with martyrdom at Uḥud, insisted upon marching out against the enemy. They pressed the case until the Prophet entered and put on his coat of armour ( لأمة ). When they saw him armed they regretted their insistence, saying: “We did badly in advising the Prophet when revelation comes to him.” They stood and apologised: “Do whatever seems best to you.” The Prophet replied: “A prophet should not put on his coat of mail and then take it off until he has fought.” [XIV-N096] He departed after the Friday prayer and was at the ravine of Uḥud by Saturday, the middle of Shawwāl, in the third year after the Hijra. He took position on one side of the valley, with Uḥud behind himself and his camp, and straightened the ranks as already described. He appointed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr to command the archers, instructing them: “Drive them off from us with arrows; do not let them reach us from behind.” Then what God decreed came to pass, as will be related subsequently. About one thousand men marched out with the Prophet for Uḥud. He had promised victory if they persevered. Upon reaching al-Shawṭ, a certain location [ Translator’s note: The Cairo print spells the place-name الشواط (al-Shawāṭ). The reading al-Shawṭ (الشوط) used here is an explicit correction, supported by Ibn al-Mulaqqin’s al-Tawḍīḥ , XXI, p. 145, in its account of Uḥud.] , Ibn Ubayy withdrew with three hundred, saying: “Why should we get ourselves killed?” Abū Jābir al-Sulamī pursued the men, imploring them by God for the sake of their Prophet and themselves. Ibn Ubayy answered, “Had we known there would be a battle, we would have followed you.” The clans of Banū Ḥāritha and Banū Salama were on the verge of turning back with him, but remained firm beside the Prophet. Thus God reminded them of His grace in the verse: “When two groups among you almost lost courage, though God was their Protector”—He supported and protected the two from following the hypocrites. Jābir said: “We would not be pleased if the verse had not been revealed, because of the words ‘God was their Protector.’” Banū Salama belonged to the Khazraj and Banū Ḥāritha to the Aws. “In God let the believers put their trust,” and not in anything else, for there is no other true Helper. [XIV-N097]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"A teacher maps the inward battle with the ego-self","html":"<p>Part of the vocation of spiritual teachers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شيوخ التربية<\/bdi>) is to guide disciples (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريدين<\/bdi>) in struggling against their own souls (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محاربة النفوس<\/bdi>), and to disclose to them the soul’s hidden plots and deceptions, thereby furnishing them with battle-stations from which to confront it. God observes their sincerity and intentions. Some grow weary, become exhausted, and return to the homeland of their habitual patterns (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وطن عوائدهم<\/bdi>); others persevere until they win the greatest booty and supreme felicity. As the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">They strove with all intensity, until most grew tired;<br/>But the one who carried on and endured embraced honour.<\/blockquote><p>Someone said: “The travelers’ journeying (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سير السائرين<\/bdi>) ends with gaining the upper hand over their own souls. If they prevail over them, they arrive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وصلوا<\/bdi>).” Some disciples experience weariness and faltering and contemplate withdrawing and turning back; then God makes them stand firm and grants them victory, and they catch up with those who patiently went before them. The chief resource of the disciple in struggling against his soul (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجاهدة النفس<\/bdi>) is reliance upon God alone (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوكل على الله والاعتماد عليه<\/bdi>). “A sign of success at the endings is returning to God at the beginnings.” “And in God let the believers put their trust.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Part of the vocation of spiritual teachers ( شيوخ التربية ) is to guide disciples ( المريدين ) in struggling against their own souls ( محاربة النفوس ), and to disclose to them the soul’s hidden plots and deceptions, thereby furnishing them with battle-stations from which to confront it. God observes their sincerity and intentions. Some grow weary, become exhausted, and return to the homeland of their habitual patterns ( وطن عوائدهم ); others persevere until they win the greatest booty and supreme felicity. As the poet says: They strove with all intensity, until most grew tired; But the one who carried on and endured embraced honour. Someone said: “The travelers’ journeying ( سير السائرين ) ends with gaining the upper hand over their own souls. If they prevail over them, they arrive ( وصلوا ).” Some disciples experience weariness and faltering and contemplate withdrawing and turning back; then God makes them stand firm and grants them victory, and they catch up with those who patiently went before them. The chief resource of the disciple in struggling against his soul ( مجاهدة النفس ) is reliance upon God alone ( التوكل على الله والاعتماد عليه ). “A sign of success at the endings is returning to God at the beginnings.” “And in God let the believers put their trust.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then reminded the people of Uḥud of the victory granted them at Badr, despite their small numbers, to strengthen their resolve.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then reminded the people of Uḥud of the victory granted them at Badr, despite their small numbers, to strengthen their resolve."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C401.1","page":401,"ar":"القدح - بالكسر -: السهم قبل أن ينصل ويراش.","en":"Qidḥ, with kasra: an arrow before its head and feathers are fitted.","anchor":"القدح"},{"id":"C401.2","page":401,"ar":"في نسخة: (الكلاب)، وكلاهما صحيح، فالكلب يجمع على كلاب وأكلب.","en":"In one copy: al-kilāb. Both forms are correct: kalb has the plurals kilāb and aklub.","anchor":"هذه الأكْلُب"},{"id":"C401.3","page":401,"ar":"الثلم: الكسر.","en":"Thalm: a break.","anchor":"ثلماً"},{"id":"C402.1","page":402,"ar":"اللأمة - مهموزة -: الدرع.","en":"Laʾma, spelled with hamza: a coat of armour.","anchor":"ولبس لأمته"},{"id":"C402.2","page":402,"ar":"عند تفسير قوله تعالى: وما محمد إلا رسول.","en":"At the explanation of God’s words, “Muḥammad is only a messenger” [3:144].","anchor":"على ما يأتي"}],"english_characters":5691,"arabic_characters":3658},{"id":"q3-123","q":"3:123","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:123 · Badr and the paradox of strength through abasement","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 402–403","cairo":"I:402–403","source_page_first":402,"source_page_last":403,"ar":"وَلَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللَّهُ بِبَدْرٍ وَأَنْتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (123)\nقلت: (بدر) : بئر بين مكة والمدينة، كانت لرجل اسمه بدر، فسميت باسم صاحبها، وقعت فيها الغزوة التي نصر الله فيها رسوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم، فسميت الغزوة باسم المكان، وجملة: (وأنتم أذلة) : حال من الكاف، و (أذلة) : جمع ذليل، كأعزة، جمع عزيزٌ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللَّهُ في وقعة بدر وَأَنْتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ ليس معكم مراكب ولا كثرة سلاح، مع قوة عدوكم بالعُدة والعدد، فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ واثبتوا مع رسوله، وانتظروا النصر من الله كما عودكم، لَعَلَّكُمْ تكونون شاكرين، لما أنعم به عليكم من العز والنصر، فيزيدكم منه كما وعدكم.\nالإشارة: جعل الله سبحانه وتعالى الأشياء كامنة في أضدادها، فمن أراد العز والنصر فليتحقق بالذل والمسكنة، ومن أراد الغنى فليتحقق بالفقر، ومن أراد الرفعة فليتحقق بالضعة وإسقاط المنزلة، ومن أراد القوة فليتحقق بالضعف، وهكذا: [تحقق بأوصافك يمدك بأوصافه] . فاتقوا الله يا معشر المريدين، واطلبوا الأشياء في أضدادها لتظفروا بها، واشكروا الله على ما أولاكم يزدكم من فضله ونواله.\nثم ذكر كيفية نصره لهم ببدر فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللَّهُ بِبَدْرٍ وَأَنْتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (123)","quran_en":"123. God certainly helped you at Badr when you were in a humbled condition. So be mindful of God, that you may give thanks.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The well of Badr and the circumstantial clause","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Badr<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدر<\/bdi>) is a well between Mecca and Madīna that belonged to a man named Badr, after whom the well was named. The expedition in which God gave His Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, victory occurred at that place; the battle was accordingly named after its location. “While you were humbled” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَأَنْتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial clause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جملة حال<\/bdi>) referring back to the object-pronoun <strong>-kum<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكاف<\/bdi>) in “He helped you.” <strong>Adhilla<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أذلة<\/bdi>) is the plural of <strong>dhalīl<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذليل<\/bdi>), just as <strong>aʿizza<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أعزة<\/bdi>) is the plural of <strong>ʿazīz<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عزيز<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N098\">[XIV-N098]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Badr ( بدر ) is a well between Mecca and Madīna that belonged to a man named Badr, after whom the well was named. The expedition in which God gave His Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, victory occurred at that place; the battle was accordingly named after its location. “While you were humbled” ( وَأَنْتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ ) is a circumstantial clause ( جملة حال ) referring back to the object-pronoun -kum ( الكاف ) in “He helped you.” Adhilla ( أذلة ) is the plural of dhalīl ( ذليل ), just as aʿizza ( أعزة ) is the plural of ʿazīz ( عزيز ). [XIV-N098]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Victory without the ordinary means of strength","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “God certainly gave you victory at Badr when you were humbled,” having neither riding animals nor much weaponry, while your enemy was strong in equipment and numbers. “So be mindful of God,” remain firm with His Messenger, and await God’s victory as He has accustomed you to do, “that you may give thanks” for the honour and triumph He has granted you, whereupon He will increase it, as He has promised.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “God certainly gave you victory at Badr when you were humbled,” having neither riding animals nor much weaponry, while your enemy was strong in equipment and numbers. “So be mindful of God,” remain firm with His Messenger, and await God’s victory as He has accustomed you to do, “that you may give thanks” for the honour and triumph He has granted you, whereupon He will increase it, as He has promised."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Created things hidden within their opposites","html":"<p>God, glorified and exalted, has placed things hidden within their contraries. Therefore whoever seeks honour and victory should realize abasement and destitution (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذل والمسكنة<\/bdi>); whoever seeks richness should realize poverty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقر<\/bdi>); whoever seeks elevation should realize lowliness and the laying aside of rank; and whoever seeks strength should realize weakness. And so it continues: “Realize your own attributes, and He will assist you with His attributes” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تحقق بأوصافك يمدك بأوصافه<\/bdi>). O company of disciples, be mindful of God and seek things in their opposites so that you may attain them; thank God for what He has bestowed and He will increase you from His grace and generosity. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N099\">[XIV-N099]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"God, glorified and exalted, has placed things hidden within their contraries. Therefore whoever seeks honour and victory should realize abasement and destitution ( الذل والمسكنة ); whoever seeks richness should realize poverty ( الفقر ); whoever seeks elevation should realize lowliness and the laying aside of rank; and whoever seeks strength should realize weakness. And so it continues: “Realize your own attributes, and He will assist you with His attributes” ( تحقق بأوصافك يمدك بأوصافه ). O company of disciples, be mindful of God and seek things in their opposites so that you may attain them; thank God for what He has bestowed and He will increase you from His grace and generosity. [XIV-N099]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The author next mentions the manner in which God assisted them at Badr.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The author next mentions the manner in which God assisted them at Badr."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1783,"arabic_characters":1041},{"id":"q3-124-125","q":"3:124–125","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:124–125 · Angelic reinforcement at Badr and the unseen aid of spiritual endeavor","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 403–404","cairo":"I:403–404","source_page_first":403,"source_page_last":404,"ar":"إِذْ تَقُولُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَلَنْ يَكْفِيَكُمْ أَنْ يُمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلاثَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ مُنْزَلِينَ (124) بَلى إِنْ تَصْبِرُوا وَتَتَّقُوا وَيَأْتُوكُمْ مِنْ فَوْرِهِمْ هذا يُمْدِدْكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِخَمْسَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ مُسَوَّمِينَ (125)\nقلت: (إذ) : ظرف لنصركم، إذا قلنا: إن الإمداد يوم بدر فقط، أو بدل من (إذ غدوت) ، إذا قلنا: كان الإمداد يوم أحد بشرط الصبر، فلما لم يصبروا لم يقع. والتسويم: التعليم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولقد نصركم الله ببدر حين كنت تَقُولُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ حين رأوا كثرة عدوهم وقلة عدتهم وعددهم: أَلَنْ يَكْفِيَكُمْ في القوة والكثرة، أَنْ يُمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلاثَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ مُنْزَلِينَ في السحاب؟ بَلى يكفيكم كما وعدكم، إِنْ تَصْبِرُوا وتثبتوا وَتَتَّقُوا الله وَيَأْتُوكُمْ مِنْ فَوْرِهِمْ أي: من سرعتهم هذا الوقت، يُمْدِدْكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِخَمْسَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ بلا تراخ ولا تأخير، مُسَوَّمِينَ أي: مُعَلَّمين بعمائم بيض إلا جبريل، فإنه كانت عمامته صفراء. أو معلمين أنفسَهم أو خيلَهم. قيل:\nكانت مجزوزة الأذناب، وقيل: كانت بُلْقاً.\nفإن قلت: ما ذكر في الأنفال إلا ألفاً، وهنا خمسة آلاف. فالجواب: أن الله تعالى أمدهم أولاً بألف، ثم صاروا ثلاثة آلاف، ثم صاروا خمسة آلاف. قال ابن عباس: لم تقاتل الملائكة إلا يوم بدر، وفيما سوى ذلك يشهدون القتال معنا، ولا يقاتلون. هـ.\nالإشارة: كل مَن توجَّه لجهاد نفسه في الله، واشتغل بذكر مولاه، أمده الله في الباطن بالأنوار والأسرار، وفي الظاهر بالملائكة الأبرار، وقد شوهد ذلك في الفقراء أصحابنا، إذا كانوا ثلاثة رآهم العامة ثلاثين، وإذا كانوا ثلاثين رأوهم ثلاثمائة، وقد كنا في سفرة سبعين، فرأونا سبعمائة على ما أخبرونا به، وَاللَّهُ يُؤَيِّدُ بِنَصْرِهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى حكمة إمداده لهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِذْ تَقُولُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَلَنْ يَكْفِيَكُمْ أَنْ يُمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلاثَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ مُنْزَلِينَ (124) بَلى إِنْ تَصْبِرُوا وَتَتَّقُوا وَيَأْتُوكُمْ مِنْ فَوْرِهِمْ هذا يُمْدِدْكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِخَمْسَةِ آلافٍ مِنَ الْمَلائِكَةِ مُسَوَّمِينَ (125)","quran_en":"124. When you said to the believers, “Will it not suffice you that your Lord should reinforce you with three thousand angels sent down?” 125. Yes indeed: if you are patient and mindful of God, and they come against you immediately, your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels bearing distinguishing marks.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The position of idh and the meaning of taswīm","html":"<p>I say: “When” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ<\/bdi>) is an adverbial of time (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظرف<\/bdi>) attached to “He helped you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نصركم<\/bdi>) if we consider the reinforcement to have occurred solely on the day of Badr. Alternatively, it is a substitute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/bdi>) for “when you went forth” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ غدوت<\/bdi>) if we consider the reinforcement to have been promised at Uḥud <i>conditional upon patience<\/i>. When they failed to remain patient, it did not take place. <strong>Taswīm<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التسويم<\/bdi>) means marking or designating (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التعليم<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N100\">[XIV-N100]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “When” ( إذ ) is an adverbial of time ( ظرف ) attached to “He helped you” ( نصركم ) if we consider the reinforcement to have occurred solely on the day of Badr. Alternatively, it is a substitute ( بدل ) for “when you went forth” ( إذ غدوت ) if we consider the reinforcement to have been promised at Uḥud conditional upon patience . When they failed to remain patient, it did not take place. Taswīm ( التسويم ) means marking or designating ( التعليم ). [XIV-N100]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The three and five thousand angels","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: God certainly helped you at Badr “when you said to the believers,” after they saw the many troops of the enemy and the fewness of their own equipment and numbers: “Will it not suffice you,” in strength and numbers, “that your Lord reinforce you with three thousand angels,” sent down within clouds? “Yes,” that will suffice you as He has promised, provided that “you are patient” and stand firm, “and are mindful of God,” and the enemy comes against you “at once” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من فورهم<\/bdi>), meaning swiftly and at this very time, then “your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels” without delay or postponement, “marked out” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مُسَوِّمِينَ<\/bdi>). They were marked with white turbans, except Gabriel, whose turban was yellow. Alternatively, they marked themselves or their horses. It is said that the horses had docked tails; or that they were piebald. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> These are differing transmitted descriptions, not a single established visual account.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N101\">[XIV-N101]<\/a><\/p><p>If you ask: Why is only one thousand mentioned in Sūrat al-Anfāl, whereas here there are five thousand? The response is that God initially reinforced them with one thousand; their number then reached three thousand; and finally it rose to five thousand. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “The angels fought only on the day of Badr; on other occasions they are present at the battle with us, but do not fight.” End of the cited report. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N101\">[XIV-N101]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: God certainly helped you at Badr “when you said to the believers,” after they saw the many troops of the enemy and the fewness of their own equipment and numbers: “Will it not suffice you,” in strength and numbers, “that your Lord reinforce you with three thousand angels,” sent down within clouds? “Yes,” that will suffice you as He has promised, provided that “you are patient” and stand firm, “and are mindful of God,” and the enemy comes against you “at once” ( من فورهم ), meaning swiftly and at this very time, then “your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels” without delay or postponement, “marked out” ( مُسَوِّمِينَ ). They were marked with white turbans, except Gabriel, whose turban was yellow. Alternatively, they marked themselves or their horses. It is said that the horses had docked tails; or that they were piebald. Translator’s note: These are differing transmitted descriptions, not a single established visual account. [XIV-N101] If you ask: Why is only one thousand mentioned in Sūrat al-Anfāl, whereas here there are five thousand? The response is that God initially reinforced them with one thousand; their number then reached three thousand; and finally it rose to five thousand. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “The angels fought only on the day of Badr; on other occasions they are present at the battle with us, but do not fight.” End of the cited report. [XIV-N101]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Hidden lights, angelic support and an episode from the author’s circle","html":"<p>Whoever turns toward struggling against his own soul for the sake of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جهاد النفس<\/bdi>), and occupies himself with the remembrance of his Master (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذكر مولاه<\/bdi>), receives from God, inwardly, assistance in lights and mysteries (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأنوار والأسرار<\/bdi>), and outwardly, the help of noble angels (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملائكة الأبرار<\/bdi>). This, Ibn ʿAjība says, has been witnessed among the spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>) of his own circle: when there were three of them, the common people saw thirty; when thirty, they saw three hundred. “We were once seventy on a journey, but people saw us as seven hundred, according to what they told us.” “And God strengthens with His help whom He wills.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N102\">[XIV-N102]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever turns toward struggling against his own soul for the sake of God ( جهاد النفس ), and occupies himself with the remembrance of his Master ( ذكر مولاه ), receives from God, inwardly, assistance in lights and mysteries ( الأنوار والأسرار ), and outwardly, the help of noble angels ( الملائكة الأبرار ). This, Ibn ʿAjība says, has been witnessed among the spiritual poor ( الفقراء ) of his own circle: when there were three of them, the common people saw thirty; when thirty, they saw three hundred. “We were once seventy on a journey, but people saw us as seven hundred, according to what they told us.” “And God strengthens with His help whom He wills.” [XIV-N102]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then explains the wisdom of that reinforcement.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then explains the wisdom of that reinforcement."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2620,"arabic_characters":1630},{"id":"q3-126-129","q":"3:126–129","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:126–129 · Victory from God, the limit of Prophetic control and the stations of effacement and subsistence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 404–405","cairo":"I:404–405","source_page_first":404,"source_page_last":405,"ar":"وَما جَعَلَهُ اللَّهُ إِلاَّ بُشْرى لَكُمْ وَلِتَطْمَئِنَّ قُلُوبُكُمْ بِهِ وَمَا النَّصْرُ إِلاَّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ الْعَزِيزِ الْحَكِيمِ (126) لِيَقْطَعَ طَرَفاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْ يَكْبِتَهُمْ فَيَنْقَلِبُوا خائِبِينَ (127) لَيْسَ لَكَ مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ أَوْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْهِمْ أَوْ يُعَذِّبَهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ ظالِمُونَ (128) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ يَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (129)\nقلت: (ليس لك من الأمر شيء) : جملة معترضة بين قوله: (أو يكبتهم) وقوله: (أو يتوب عليهم) ، أو تكون (أو) بمعنى (إلا) ، أي: ليس لك من الأمر شيء، إلا أن يتوب عليهم فتبشرهم، أو يعذبهم فتتشفى فيهم. قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وما جعل الله ذلك الإمداد إلا بشارة لكم بالنصر، وَلِتَطْمَئِنَّ قُلُوبُكُمْ بِهِ فتثبتوا للقتال، وَمَا النَّصْرُ إِلَّا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ فهو قادر على أن ينصركم بلا واسطة، لكن أراد أن يثيبكم وينسب المزية إليكم، حيث قتلهم على أيديكم، فإن الله عزيز لا يغلب، حكيم فيما دبر وأبرم، وإنما نصركم يوم بدر لِيَقْطَعَ طَرَفاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا بقتل بعض وأسر آخرين، فإنه قتل يومئذ سبعون، وأسر سبعون، أَوْ يَكْبِتَهُمْ أي: يحزنهم ويغيظهم، والكبت: شدة الغيظ، فَيَنْقَلِبُوا خائِبِينَ مما أملوا.\nولما جرح- عليه الصلاة والسلام- في وجهه، وشُجَّ على قرن حاجبه، وكُسِرَت رباعيته، هَمَّ بالدعاء على الكفار، بل دعا عليهم، فأنزل الله: لَيْسَ لَكَ مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ إنما أنت رسول إليهم، مأمور بإنذارهم وجهادهم، وأمرهم بيد مالكهم، إن شاء هداهم وإن شاء عذّبهم. وإنما نهاه عن الدعاء عليهم لعلمه بأن منهم من يُسلم ويجاهد في سبيل الله، وقد كان كذلك فجُلَّهم أسلموا وجاهدوا، منهم خالد بن الوليد- سيف الله فى أرضه.\nثم عطف على قوله: لِيَقْطَعَ طَرَفاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْ يَكْبِتَهُمْ قوله: أَوْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْهِمْ إن أسلموا أَوْ يُعَذِّبَهُمْ إن لم يسلموا، فَإِنَّهُمْ ظالِمُونَ قد استحقوا العذاب بظلمهم، والأمور كلها بيد الله، وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ خلقا وملكاً وعبيداً، يَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ غفرانه، وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ تعذيبه، ولا يجب عليه شيء، وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ لعباده، فلا تبادر بالدعاء عليهم.\nالإشارة: وما جعل الله التأييد الذي ينزله على أهل التجريد، حين يقابلهم بالابتلاء والتشديد، إذا أراد أن يوصلهم لصفاء التوحيد، إلا بشارة لفتحهم، ولتطمئن بمعرفته قلوبهم، فإن الامتكان على قدر الامتحان، وكل محنة تزيد مكنة، وهذه سُنة الله في أوليائه يسلط عليهم الخلق في بدايتهم، ويشدد عليهم البلاء، حتى إذا طهروا من البقايا، وكملت فيهم المزايا، كف عنهم الأذى، وانقلب الجلال جمالاً، وذلك اعتناء بهم، ونصراً لهم على أنفسهم، فإن النصر كله مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ الْعَزِيزِ الْحَكِيمِ. وذلك ليقطع عنهم طرفاً من الشواغل والعلائق، التي تقبضهم عن العروج إلى سماء الحقائق، فإن الروح إذا رقدت في ظل العز والجاه صعب خروجها من هذا العالم، فإذا ضيق عليها، وعكس مرادها، رحلت إلى عالم الملكوت، والأمر كله بيد الله. ليس لك أيها الفقير من الأمر شيء، إنما أنت مأمور بتحريك الأسباب(1) والله يفتح الباب. وليس لك أيها الشيخ من الأمر شيء، إنما أنت مذكر، وعلى الله البلاغ، فلا تأس على ما فاتك، ولا تفرح بما آتاك، فملكوت السموات والأرض بيد الله، يَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ.\nقال القشيري: جرَّده- أي: نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلم لما به عرفه عن كُلِّ غيْرٍ وسبب، حيث أخبره أنه ليس له من الأمر شيء، ثم قال: ويقال: أقامه في وقتٍ مقاماً رمى بقبضة من التراب، فأصابت جميع الوجوه، وقال: وَما رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ رَمى وقال في وقت آخر: لَيْسَ لَكَ مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ. هـ.\nيشير إلى أنهما مقامان: نيابة عن الله بالله، ونيابة الله عن عبده، والأول بقاء، والثاني فناء، قاله المحشى.\nقلت: الأول في مقام البسط، والثاني في مقام القبض، فقد قالوا: إذا بسط فلا فاقة، وإذا قبض فلا طاقة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما كان النصر فى الجهاد لا يكون إلا بأكل الحلال وطاعة الكبير المتعال، قدّم ذكر ذلك قبل الأمر بالقتال فى قضية أحد، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَما جَعَلَهُ اللَّهُ إِلاَّ بُشْرى لَكُمْ وَلِتَطْمَئِنَّ قُلُوبُكُمْ بِهِ وَمَا النَّصْرُ إِلاَّ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ الْعَزِيزِ الْحَكِيمِ (126) لِيَقْطَعَ طَرَفاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْ يَكْبِتَهُمْ فَيَنْقَلِبُوا خائِبِينَ (127) لَيْسَ لَكَ مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ أَوْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْهِمْ أَوْ يُعَذِّبَهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ ظالِمُونَ (128) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ يَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (129)","quran_en":"126. God made it only glad tidings for you, so that your hearts might be at rest through it. Victory is only from God, the Mighty, the Wise—127. that He might cut off a portion of those who disbelieve, or suppress them so that they turn back frustrated. 128. You have no part in deciding the matter, whether He turns toward them in mercy or chastises them; they are wrongdoers. 129. To God belongs whatsoever is in the heavens and whatsoever is on the earth. He forgives whom He wills and chastises whom He wills; God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"An inserted clause between coordinated possibilities","html":"<p>I say: “You have no part in the matter” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ليس لك من الأمر شيء<\/bdi>) is a parenthetical clause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جملة معترضة<\/bdi>) inserted between “or suppress them” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أو يكبتهم<\/bdi>) and “or turn toward them” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أو يتوب عليهم<\/bdi>). Alternatively, the particle <strong>aw<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أو<\/bdi>) may carry the force of “except” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إلا<\/bdi>): “You have no part in the matter, except that if He accepts their repentance you may bring them glad tidings, or if He punishes them you may take satisfaction in their punishment.” This second grammatical account is attributed to al-Bayḍāwī. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N103\">[XIV-N103]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “You have no part in the matter” ( ليس لك من الأمر شيء ) is a parenthetical clause ( جملة معترضة ) inserted between “or suppress them” ( أو يكبتهم ) and “or turn toward them” ( أو يتوب عليهم ). Alternatively, the particle aw ( أو ) may carry the force of “except” ( إلا ): “You have no part in the matter, except that if He accepts their repentance you may bring them glad tidings, or if He punishes them you may take satisfaction in their punishment.” This second grammatical account is attributed to al-Bayḍāwī. [XIV-N103]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The means of victory and the Prophet’s relation to his enemies","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: God made the reinforcement only “glad tidings” of victory, so that your hearts might rest secure through it and you stand firm in battle. “Victory is only from God”: He can give you victory without any intermediary, but He desired to reward you and to ascribe distinction to you by causing the enemies to be killed at your hands. He is Mighty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عزيز<\/bdi>), none can overcome Him, and Wise (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حكيم<\/bdi>) in what He determines and orders. He gave victory at Badr “to cut off a portion of those who disbelieve,” killing some and taking others captive—seventy, he reports, being killed that day and seventy taken prisoner—or “to suppress them,” distressing and enraging them. <strong>Kabṭ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكبت<\/bdi>) is intense rage: “so they return frustrated,” their hopes unfulfilled.<\/p><p>When the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was wounded in the face, cut near his eyebrow and suffered a broken front tooth, he intended to pray against the unbelievers—indeed, he prayed against them. God then revealed: “You have no part in the matter.” You are only a messenger sent to them, charged with warning them and struggling against them, while their destiny rests in the hands of their Owner. If He wills, He guides them; if He wills, He punishes them. God forbade him to call down punishment on them because He knew that some of them would accept Islam and strive in God’s way. Ibn ʿAjība says that this did happen: most entered Islam and fought in its cause, including Khālid ibn al-Walīd, “God’s sword upon His earth.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N104\">[XIV-N104]<\/a><\/p><p>The phrase “or He turns toward them in mercy” is then coordinated with “to cut off a portion of those who disbelieve or suppress them”: He may accept their repentance if they submit, “or chastise them” if they do not, “for they are wrongdoers,” having incurred punishment through their injustice. All matters rest in God’s hands: “To God belongs whatsoever is in the heavens and whatsoever is on the earth,” by creation, ownership and servitude. “He forgives whom He wills” to forgive “and chastises whom He wills” to chastise; nothing is incumbent upon Him. “God is Forgiving, Merciful” to His servants, so do not hasten to call down punishment on them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: God made the reinforcement only “glad tidings” of victory, so that your hearts might rest secure through it and you stand firm in battle. “Victory is only from God”: He can give you victory without any intermediary, but He desired to reward you and to ascribe distinction to you by causing the enemies to be killed at your hands. He is Mighty ( عزيز ), none can overcome Him, and Wise ( حكيم ) in what He determines and orders. He gave victory at Badr “to cut off a portion of those who disbelieve,” killing some and taking others captive—seventy, he reports, being killed that day and seventy taken prisoner—or “to suppress them,” distressing and enraging them. Kabṭ ( الكبت ) is intense rage: “so they return frustrated,” their hopes unfulfilled. When the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was wounded in the face, cut near his eyebrow and suffered a broken front tooth, he intended to pray against the unbelievers—indeed, he prayed against them. God then revealed: “You have no part in the matter.” You are only a messenger sent to them, charged with warning them and struggling against them, while their destiny rests in the hands of their Owner. If He wills, He guides them; if He wills, He punishes them. God forbade him to call down punishment on them because He knew that some of them would accept Islam and strive in God’s way. Ibn ʿAjība says that this did happen: most entered Islam and fought in its cause, including Khālid ibn al-Walīd, “God’s sword upon His earth.” [XIV-N104] The phrase “or He turns toward them in mercy” is then coordinated with “to cut off a portion of those who disbelieve or suppress them”: He may accept their repentance if they submit, “or chastise them” if they do not, “for they are wrongdoers,” having incurred punishment through their injustice. All matters rest in God’s hands: “To God belongs whatsoever is in the heavens and whatsoever is on the earth,” by creation, ownership and servitude. “He forgives whom He wills” to forgive “and chastises whom He wills” to chastise; nothing is incumbent upon Him. “God is Forgiving, Merciful” to His servants, so do not hasten to call down punishment on them."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Affliction as preparation for freedom from dependence","html":"<p>The support (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التأييد<\/bdi>) that God sends down upon the people of detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التجريد<\/bdi>), when He confronts them with trial and hardship in order to bring them to the purity of tawḥīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفاء التوحيد<\/bdi>), is only glad tidings of their spiritual opening (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فتح<\/bdi>), and serves to give their hearts repose in knowing Him. Secure establishment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الامتكان<\/bdi>) comes in proportion to testing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الامتحان<\/bdi>); every ordeal adds to one’s capacity to stand firm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مكنة<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>This is God’s practice with His friends: at their beginnings He grants other creatures power over them and intensifies their trials until they have been purified of their residues (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البقايا<\/bdi>) and their merits have reached completion. Then He restrains the harm, and Majesty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجلال<\/bdi>) turns to Beauty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمال<\/bdi>). This is His solicitude for them and His victory for them over their own souls. All victory comes from “God, the Mighty, the Wise.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N105\">[XIV-N105]<\/a><\/p><p>He does this “to cut off” from them some of the distractions and attachments (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشواغل والعلائق<\/bdi>) that keep them from rising to the heaven of realities (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سماء الحقائق<\/bdi>). When the spirit lies at ease in the shade of honor and status (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العز والجاه<\/bdi>), it finds it hard to leave this world; when constrained and thwarted, it travels to the world of Dominion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملكوت<\/bdi>). All matters are in God’s hands. You, spiritual poor one, “have no part in the matter”: you are commanded to set the means in motion, while God opens the door. You, spiritual teacher, likewise “have no part in the matter”: you only give reminder, while bringing [them] to the goal rests with God. Neither grieve over what has escaped you nor exult over what He has given you. The Dominion of the heavens and earth is God’s: “He forgives whom He wills and chastises whom He wills; God is Forgiving, Merciful.”<\/p><p><span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The printed phrase <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وعلى الله البلاغ<\/bdi> is retained. Balāgh can denote reaching an end or bringing something to it, as well as conveying a message (Ibn Manẓūr, Lisān al-ʿArab, root b-l-gh, II:144). The present rendering follows the immediate context: the disciple sets the means in motion, God opens the door, and the teacher gives reminder. The same idiom appears in a travel anecdote quoted by Ibn al-Jawzī, <i>Talbīs Iblīs<\/i>, I:290: “Walking is my part; bringing me there is God’s.” This is linguistic corroboration, not a claim that the anecdote is authenticated or is Ibn ʿAjība’s source. The supplied object “[them]” makes the contextual interpretation visible; no emendation of the Arabic is proposed.]<\/span><\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī says: “He stripped him”—the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him—“of every other thing and cause, once He had made Himself known to him, telling him that he had no part in the matter.” Then he says: “It is also said that at one moment He established him in a station in which he cast a handful of dust that struck every face, and God said, ‘You did not throw when you threw, but God threw’ [Q 8:17]. At another moment He said, ‘You have no part in the matter.’” End of quotation.<\/p><p>The glossator explains that these indicate two stations: acting as God’s deputy <i>through God<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نيابة عن الله بالله<\/bdi>), and God acting in the place of His servant (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نيابة الله عن عبده<\/bdi>). The first is subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بقاء<\/bdi>), the second effacement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فناء<\/bdi>), according to the gloss. I say: The first belongs to the station of expansion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البسط<\/bdi>), and the second to that of contraction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القبض<\/bdi>). Thus they have said: “When He expands [the heart], there is no destitution; when He contracts it, there is no power.” God Most High knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N106\">[XIV-N106]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The support ( التأييد ) that God sends down upon the people of detachment ( أهل التجريد ), when He confronts them with trial and hardship in order to bring them to the purity of tawḥīd ( صفاء التوحيد ), is only glad tidings of their spiritual opening ( فتح ), and serves to give their hearts repose in knowing Him. Secure establishment ( الامتكان ) comes in proportion to testing ( الامتحان ); every ordeal adds to one’s capacity to stand firm ( مكنة ). This is God’s practice with His friends: at their beginnings He grants other creatures power over them and intensifies their trials until they have been purified of their residues ( البقايا ) and their merits have reached completion. Then He restrains the harm, and Majesty ( الجلال ) turns to Beauty ( الجمال ). This is His solicitude for them and His victory for them over their own souls. All victory comes from “God, the Mighty, the Wise.” [XIV-N105] He does this “to cut off” from them some of the distractions and attachments ( الشواغل والعلائق ) that keep them from rising to the heaven of realities ( سماء الحقائق ). When the spirit lies at ease in the shade of honor and status ( العز والجاه ), it finds it hard to leave this world; when constrained and thwarted, it travels to the world of Dominion ( عالم الملكوت ). All matters are in God’s hands. You, spiritual poor one, “have no part in the matter”: you are commanded to set the means in motion, while God opens the door. You, spiritual teacher, likewise “have no part in the matter”: you only give reminder, while bringing [them] to the goal rests with God. Neither grieve over what has escaped you nor exult over what He has given you. The Dominion of the heavens and earth is God’s: “He forgives whom He wills and chastises whom He wills; God is Forgiving, Merciful.” [Translator’s note: The printed phrase وعلى الله البلاغ is retained. Balāgh can denote reaching an end or bringing something to it, as well as conveying a message (Ibn Manẓūr, Lisān al-ʿArab, root b-l-gh, II:144). The present rendering follows the immediate context: the disciple sets the means in motion, God opens the door, and the teacher gives reminder. The same idiom appears in a travel anecdote quoted by Ibn al-Jawzī, Talbīs Iblīs , I:290: “Walking is my part; bringing me there is God’s.” This is linguistic corroboration, not a claim that the anecdote is authenticated or is Ibn ʿAjība’s source. The supplied object “[them]” makes the contextual interpretation visible; no emendation of the Arabic is proposed.] Al-Qushayrī says: “He stripped him”—the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him—“of every other thing and cause, once He had made Himself known to him, telling him that he had no part in the matter.” Then he says: “It is also said that at one moment He established him in a station in which he cast a handful of dust that struck every face, and God said, ‘You did not throw when you threw, but God threw’ [Q 8:17]. At another moment He said, ‘You have no part in the matter.’” End of quotation. The glossator explains that these indicate two stations: acting as God’s deputy through God ( نيابة عن الله بالله ), and God acting in the place of His servant ( نيابة الله عن عبده ). The first is subsistence ( بقاء ), the second effacement ( فناء ), according to the gloss. I say: The first belongs to the station of expansion ( البسط ), and the second to that of contraction ( القبض ). Thus they have said: “When He expands [the heart], there is no destitution; when He contracts it, there is no power.” God Most High knows best. [XIV-N106]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Since victory in striving is attained only through consuming the lawful (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أكل الحلال<\/bdi>) and obedience to the Most High, he introduces these matters before returning to the command to fight in connection with Uḥud.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Since victory in striving is attained only through consuming the lawful ( أكل الحلال ) and obedience to the Most High, he introduces these matters before returning to the command to fight in connection with Uḥud."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C405.1","page":405,"ar":"في «أ» السبب.","en":"In [copy] A: “the means” (al-sabab), singular.","anchor":"بتحريك الأسباب"}],"english_characters":6485,"arabic_characters":3658}];
